WEBVTT

1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,000
(suspenseful string music plays)

2
00:00:28,625 --> 00:00:30,167
(sighs)

3
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:32,208
(fire crackling)

4
00:00:38,792 --> 00:00:40,625
(man) <i>Hard to know where to start.</i>

5
00:00:41,833 --> 00:00:45,250
<i>I guess to tell the story
of the Good Friday murder through my eyes,</i>

6
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:46,958
<i>I got to start here.</i>

7
00:00:47,625 --> 00:00:51,750
<i>Nine months ago, when this asshole deacon
said something way out of line,</i>

8
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:53,042
<i>and I did this.</i>

9
00:00:53,125 --> 00:00:55,500
- (imperceptible)
- (grunts)

10
00:00:56,042 --> 00:00:57,208
- (thuds)
- (priest gasps)

11
00:00:58,333 --> 00:00:59,958
Oh, shit.

12
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:03,083
So, you're a fighter, then?

13
00:01:03,583 --> 00:01:05,708
Uh, no, Father, absolutely not.

14
00:01:05,792 --> 00:01:09,042
Well, we have a deacon who would say
otherwise if his jaw wasn't broken.

15
00:01:09,125 --> 00:01:11,750
In my previous life, yes, I was a boxer,

16
00:01:12,250 --> 00:01:14,833
and I lived on the streets.
I did some other things.

17
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:18,708
We need fighters today,
but to fight the world,

18
00:01:19,458 --> 00:01:21,083
not ourselves.

19
00:01:21,167 --> 00:01:24,625
A priest is a shepherd.
The world is a wolf.

20
00:01:24,708 --> 00:01:25,833
No.

21
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:33,625
(sighs softly) I… I don't believe that,
Father, respectfully.

22
00:01:33,708 --> 00:01:36,500
(hesitates) You start fighting wolves,
and before you know it,

23
00:01:36,583 --> 00:01:38,292
everyone you don't understand is a wolf.

24
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:42,333
I still got that fighting instinct,
and I gave into it today,

25
00:01:42,417 --> 00:01:44,833
but Christ came to heal the world,
not fight it.

26
00:01:44,917 --> 00:01:46,208
I believe that.

27
00:01:46,292 --> 00:01:49,583
It's this, not this, you know?
(inhales deeply)

28
00:01:50,833 --> 00:01:52,667
I just want to be a good priest.

29
00:01:53,625 --> 00:01:56,917
Show broken people like me
the forgiveness and love of Christ.

30
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
The world needs that so bad.

31
00:02:01,708 --> 00:02:04,583
You give me one more shot,
and I promise I'll do that.

32
00:02:05,083 --> 00:02:07,333
(church bell tolling in distance)

33
00:02:11,333 --> 00:02:14,000
- (church bell continues tolling)
- (vehicle horn honks)

34
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:16,875
(tolling stops)

35
00:02:16,958 --> 00:02:18,125
Your Excellency,

36
00:02:18,833 --> 00:02:21,042
you stuck your neck out for me
so many times.

37
00:02:21,125 --> 00:02:23,625
- I let you down, and whatever the...
- All right.

38
00:02:23,708 --> 00:02:24,792
Listen…

39
00:02:25,292 --> 00:02:28,250
Deacon Clark is famously a dick.

40
00:02:28,333 --> 00:02:30,792
Nobody's actually that upset
that you clocked him.

41
00:02:30,875 --> 00:02:32,375
In fact, kind of the opposite.

42
00:02:32,458 --> 00:02:35,042
- But we do need to do something about it.
- (exhales)

43
00:02:36,042 --> 00:02:39,250
We're sending you
to a small parish in Chimney Rock.

44
00:02:39,333 --> 00:02:42,375
It's just one priest there now.

45
00:02:42,458 --> 00:02:43,583
(wind chime tinkling)

46
00:02:43,667 --> 00:02:45,000
Assistant pastor?

47
00:02:45,083 --> 00:02:47,250
Yeah, well, curb your enthusiasm.

48
00:02:47,333 --> 00:02:48,333
What?

49
00:02:48,667 --> 00:02:53,417
(sighs) You're going
to Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude,

50
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:56,583
led by Monsignor Jefferson Wicks.
Have you heard of Jefferson Wicks?

51
00:02:57,458 --> 00:03:00,958
Okay, look, Wicks has his supporters here.
I'm not one of them.

52
00:03:01,042 --> 00:03:02,125
Between you and me,

53
00:03:02,208 --> 00:03:05,167
I think he's a few beads shy of
a full rosary and a real son of a bitch.

54
00:03:05,250 --> 00:03:06,083
(chuckles)

55
00:03:06,167 --> 00:03:10,833
But what's undeniable is that his flock
is shrinking and even calcifying.

56
00:03:11,792 --> 00:03:15,333
It could use some of what
you said in there. You understand?

57
00:03:15,958 --> 00:03:18,292
Not at all, but yes.

58
00:03:18,375 --> 00:03:20,458
Yes, yes, yes, yes, yes.

59
00:03:20,542 --> 00:03:23,417
- Spirit's got me. Let me at him.
- Hey. Hey, hey.

60
00:03:23,500 --> 00:03:27,042
This, not this, right? Mm?

61
00:03:31,167 --> 00:03:32,375
Good luck, kid.

62
00:03:33,042 --> 00:03:35,250
(intriguing music plays)

63
00:03:38,167 --> 00:03:40,792
(Jud) <i>So, that's how I came</i>
<i>to Chimney Rock.</i>

64
00:03:43,333 --> 00:03:45,167
<i>Daniel in the lion's den.</i>

65
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:47,000
<i>David facing off Goliath.</i>

66
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,917
<i>Young, dumb, and full of Christ.</i>

67
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:51,750
<i>Ready for anything.</i>

68
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:54,208
(door creaks, closes)

69
00:03:54,292 --> 00:03:56,292
(intriguing music continues)

70
00:03:59,250 --> 00:04:00,292
(bag thuds)

71
00:04:04,083 --> 00:04:05,083
(music fades)

72
00:04:09,875 --> 00:04:12,292
(door creaking)

73
00:04:23,792 --> 00:04:24,875
Father Jefferson?

74
00:04:25,792 --> 00:04:28,083
Hello. Jud Duplenticy from Albany?

75
00:04:28,167 --> 00:04:30,875
Lord be with you,
Jud Duplenticy from Albany.

76
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,125
You here to take my church away from me?

77
00:04:37,458 --> 00:04:39,250
(chuckling) Oh. No.

78
00:04:39,833 --> 00:04:40,833
Good.

79
00:04:41,583 --> 00:04:44,000
Okay, call me Monsignor Wicks.

80
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:46,208
I see you've met Martha.

81
00:04:47,083 --> 00:04:48,333
Martha? No.

82
00:04:48,417 --> 00:04:49,917
- Monsignor Wicks.
- Jesus!

83
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,500
- I woke up early to polish the silver.
- (sighs)

84
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:53,875
It was a bit blotchy.

85
00:04:53,958 --> 00:04:55,500
That'll be fine, Martha.

86
00:04:55,583 --> 00:04:57,792
Father… Jud.

87
00:04:59,083 --> 00:05:00,375
You are welcome here.

88
00:05:00,875 --> 00:05:02,500
- Thank you, Martha.
- Mm.

89
00:05:02,583 --> 00:05:05,208
I was actually
just saying to Father Wicks…

90
00:05:05,292 --> 00:05:06,875
Monsignor Wicks.

91
00:05:07,708 --> 00:05:09,583
Monsignor, right. Sorry. (Chuckles)

92
00:05:10,083 --> 00:05:11,708
And sorry for saying "Jesus".

93
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:15,667
Hoo. (Chuckles)
This is going great, I think.

94
00:05:15,750 --> 00:05:18,292
- Bishop Langstrom sent you, huh?
- Mm-hmm.

95
00:05:19,167 --> 00:05:20,250
Langstrom.

96
00:05:20,750 --> 00:05:22,417
- I know him well.
- (chuckles)

97
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:24,708
He handpicked you, sent you here.

98
00:05:25,500 --> 00:05:27,000
It says something to me.

99
00:05:29,583 --> 00:05:30,792
Tells me a lot.

100
00:05:31,917 --> 00:05:34,708
Well, I… I know
you're used to flying solo, but, um…

101
00:05:35,292 --> 00:05:36,917
I'm… I'm here to serve.

102
00:05:38,417 --> 00:05:39,625
Take my confession.

103
00:05:41,292 --> 00:05:43,083
(Wicks groans) All right.

104
00:05:43,792 --> 00:05:45,083
- Mm.
- (chuckles)

105
00:05:47,167 --> 00:05:49,708
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.

106
00:05:49,792 --> 00:05:51,000
It's been, uh,

107
00:05:51,875 --> 00:05:54,292
six weeks since my last confession.

108
00:05:55,042 --> 00:05:57,083
(inhales deeply, sighs)

109
00:05:58,708 --> 00:06:01,250
I've envied the material wealth of others.

110
00:06:02,125 --> 00:06:06,750
I saw this luxury car commercial.
Sam had it on his TV. Lexus.

111
00:06:07,417 --> 00:06:08,625
And I thought,

112
00:06:09,125 --> 00:06:11,875
- "Ah! That is a good-looking car."
- (both chuckle)

113
00:06:11,958 --> 00:06:13,458
The coupe.

114
00:06:14,583 --> 00:06:17,958
Uh… I've envied the power of great men.

115
00:06:19,375 --> 00:06:22,750
Envied my grandfather's power as a priest.
I wanted that.

116
00:06:22,833 --> 00:06:24,042
Always did.

117
00:06:25,792 --> 00:06:26,792
Uh…

118
00:06:27,542 --> 00:06:28,958
I've masturbated,

119
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:33,708
um, four times this week.

120
00:06:33,792 --> 00:06:36,625
Four or five, generally, in…
What'd I say, six weeks?

121
00:06:36,708 --> 00:06:39,792
Well, let's say 30 times, masturbated.

122
00:06:40,833 --> 00:06:44,458
Uh, this week, in my bed,
in the morning once.

123
00:06:44,958 --> 00:06:49,042
Once in the shower, standing up,
which was convenient.

124
00:06:49,125 --> 00:06:51,250
Just use a bath gel.

125
00:06:51,333 --> 00:06:52,250
(inhales deeply)

126
00:06:52,333 --> 00:06:55,542
Oh, once in the middle of the night,
after a dream about...

127
00:06:55,625 --> 00:06:58,208
- That's all right.
- About one of those Japanese cat cafés.

128
00:06:58,292 --> 00:07:00,625
- Okay.
- You know, I read this article,

129
00:07:00,708 --> 00:07:05,917
but the cats were girls,
and, you know, so…

130
00:07:06,583 --> 00:07:08,000
And I hadn't prepared,

131
00:07:08,083 --> 00:07:12,125
so I had to finish into a copy
of <i>Catholic Chronicle </i>magazine,

132
00:07:12,833 --> 00:07:16,917
just what was on the end table,
which is probably its own kind of sin.

133
00:07:17,417 --> 00:07:20,417
I don't know, maybe not, but…
Not good. (Chuckles)

134
00:07:21,542 --> 00:07:23,833
(Jud) <i>At the time,</i>
<i>I thought this was just weird.</i>

135
00:07:23,917 --> 00:07:24,750
(imperceptible)

136
00:07:24,833 --> 00:07:26,917
<i>But looking back now, I know.</i>

137
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:29,333
<i>This was Wicks' first punch.</i>

138
00:07:29,417 --> 00:07:31,458
Five Hail Marys and one Glory Be.

139
00:07:33,167 --> 00:07:34,458
Thank you, Father.

140
00:07:40,042 --> 00:07:41,542
(Jud) <i>It wouldn't be his last.</i>

141
00:07:41,625 --> 00:07:42,875
(Wicks) Oh, and welcome

142
00:07:43,667 --> 00:07:45,083
to <i>my </i>church.

143
00:07:45,917 --> 00:07:48,667
- (door opens, closes)
- (sighs)

144
00:07:48,750 --> 00:07:51,208
<i>Over the next few weeks, I settled in</i>

145
00:07:51,292 --> 00:07:53,500
<i>at Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude.</i>

146
00:07:54,333 --> 00:07:56,667
(intriguing music plays)

147
00:07:59,167 --> 00:08:03,792
<i>The only other full-time employee
was the groundskeeper, Samson.</i>

148
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,583
<i>Sam.</i>

149
00:08:11,125 --> 00:08:12,833
(Sam) <i>It's Monsignor Wicks</i>

150
00:08:12,917 --> 00:08:17,000
who gives me the strength every day
to not go back to the bottle.

151
00:08:17,917 --> 00:08:19,042
He used to drink too.

152
00:08:19,125 --> 00:08:20,042
(music fades)

153
00:08:20,125 --> 00:08:24,083
But he said to me once,
"If I can stave off that demon, you can."

154
00:08:25,375 --> 00:08:27,292
And every day, it is a struggle.

155
00:08:28,167 --> 00:08:29,250
But I have.

156
00:08:30,542 --> 00:08:34,042
I credit him and… my sweet Martha.

157
00:08:34,125 --> 00:08:36,625
Your… your sweet Martha?

158
00:08:36,708 --> 00:08:38,333
I'd do anything for her.

159
00:08:39,708 --> 00:08:42,083
- My angel on Earth.
- (organ music playing)

160
00:08:42,167 --> 00:08:45,458
(Jud) <i>As far as the church goes,</i>
<i>Martha does it all.</i>

161
00:08:45,542 --> 00:08:48,750
<i>She keeps the books,
manages donations, files everything.</i>

162
00:08:48,833 --> 00:08:49,958
File that.

163
00:08:50,542 --> 00:08:52,958
<i>She launders the vestments,
stocks the supplies,</i>

164
00:08:53,042 --> 00:08:55,167
<i>feeds Wicks, plays the organ.</i>

165
00:08:58,875 --> 00:09:00,792
<i>She knew where the bodies were buried.</i>

166
00:09:01,583 --> 00:09:03,667
- So this is the crypt?
- (Sam) Yep.

167
00:09:03,750 --> 00:09:06,708
It's a damn shame.
Gotta put up a security camera.

168
00:09:07,208 --> 00:09:09,333
- (Jud) Is there an entrance?
- There is.

169
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:11,708
This right here,

170
00:09:12,208 --> 00:09:13,333
it's a Lazarus door.

171
00:09:14,042 --> 00:09:16,917
Takes construction equipment
to open from the outside.

172
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:19,875
But cantilevered as such that one push

173
00:09:20,458 --> 00:09:22,875
will send it tumbling
to the ground from inside.

174
00:09:24,125 --> 00:09:25,458
Who's in there then?

175
00:09:25,542 --> 00:09:26,542
- Prentice.
- (gasps)

176
00:09:27,167 --> 00:09:30,625
Wicks' grandfather,
the founder of this church.

177
00:09:31,500 --> 00:09:33,000
Was like a father to me.

178
00:09:33,667 --> 00:09:35,667
Makes me sick.

179
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:40,000
These kids painting rocket ships
all over his sacred resting place.

180
00:09:46,708 --> 00:09:49,417
- (Wicks) What are you doing?
- Oh, uh…

181
00:09:50,208 --> 00:09:52,125
Well, I do a little wood-working,

182
00:09:52,208 --> 00:09:55,708
so thought I could borrow
some of Sam's tools, make a proper...

183
00:09:55,792 --> 00:09:57,000
No, we leave that.

184
00:09:57,083 --> 00:09:59,958
A reminder of the shameful sin
of the harlot whore.

185
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:02,917
Take my confession.

186
00:10:04,875 --> 00:10:06,625
(Martha) Yes, the harlot whore.

187
00:10:08,125 --> 00:10:09,625
That was Wicks' mother.

188
00:10:09,708 --> 00:10:12,333
Okay, so what's the deal with that?

189
00:10:12,417 --> 00:10:14,625
She was a harlot and a whore.

190
00:10:15,458 --> 00:10:16,292
Okay.

191
00:10:16,375 --> 00:10:19,583
When Prentice entered the priesthood
and founded this church,

192
00:10:19,667 --> 00:10:21,833
he was widowed with a daughter.

193
00:10:22,958 --> 00:10:24,542
Grace was her name.

194
00:10:25,125 --> 00:10:26,750
Always a bad seed.

195
00:10:26,833 --> 00:10:32,167
She loved revealing clothes
and her fancy brands.

196
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:33,875
Fancy brands… Oof.

197
00:10:33,958 --> 00:10:35,042
- Mm.
- Yeah.

198
00:10:35,125 --> 00:10:37,958
As a teenager, she slutted around bars,

199
00:10:38,042 --> 00:10:40,250
was soon pregnant by a drifter.

200
00:10:40,333 --> 00:10:41,167
(imperceptible)

201
00:10:41,250 --> 00:10:45,292
(Martha) <i>Prentice had</i>
<i>a vast family fortune in the bank.</i>

202
00:10:45,375 --> 00:10:48,417
- (intriguing music plays)
<i>- To protect his grandson, Wicks,</i>

203
00:10:48,500 --> 00:10:53,083
<i>he promised that
if Grace stayed under his roof,</i>

204
00:10:53,792 --> 00:10:56,917
the fortune would be willed to her.

205
00:10:57,917 --> 00:11:01,750
<i>And so the whore
waited for her father to die.</i>

206
00:11:02,667 --> 00:11:05,083
<i>Oh, it weighed on him heavily.</i>

207
00:11:05,167 --> 00:11:06,667
Martha, remember this.

208
00:11:07,833 --> 00:11:10,667
Wealth and the power that comes with it,

209
00:11:11,417 --> 00:11:12,667
is Eve's Apple.

210
00:11:13,917 --> 00:11:16,042
Temptation that leads to the fall.

211
00:11:17,292 --> 00:11:21,125
I must protect our loved ones
from its corrupting influence,

212
00:11:22,208 --> 00:11:23,625
at all costs.

213
00:11:24,375 --> 00:11:26,208
<i>His day came at last.</i>

214
00:11:26,292 --> 00:11:27,542
I was there.

215
00:11:28,625 --> 00:11:31,833
<i>I saw Prentice take his final communion</i>

216
00:11:31,917 --> 00:11:34,667
<i>and die on the holy altar.</i>

217
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:36,167
<i>At peace.</i>

218
00:11:36,958 --> 00:11:40,458
The harlot whore
went straight to Prentice's attorney.

219
00:11:41,042 --> 00:11:44,125
"Give me my money," she said.

220
00:11:45,375 --> 00:11:46,833
And do you know what he said?

221
00:11:47,958 --> 00:11:52,458
"Yes, you are heir
to every single penny Prentice had."

222
00:11:53,250 --> 00:11:54,875
And in his accounts,

223
00:11:56,167 --> 00:11:59,167
not one hot dime.

224
00:11:59,875 --> 00:12:02,042
Mm, what did he do with the fortune?

225
00:12:02,125 --> 00:12:04,042
Gave it to the poor, say some.

226
00:12:04,667 --> 00:12:07,542
Threw it in the ocean, say others.
No one knows.

227
00:12:08,417 --> 00:12:09,417
It was gone.

228
00:12:10,375 --> 00:12:14,208
That holy man's final act of grace

229
00:12:15,333 --> 00:12:19,625
was to keep the corrupting evil
out of wicked hands.

230
00:12:20,667 --> 00:12:22,500
All that he left her…

231
00:12:25,542 --> 00:12:26,542
Was this.

232
00:12:27,167 --> 00:12:29,208
"What is this?" she said.

233
00:12:30,292 --> 00:12:31,708
But I knew.

234
00:12:32,417 --> 00:12:34,250
Look not for Eve's Apple.

235
00:12:34,917 --> 00:12:38,333
Your inheritance is now Christ.

236
00:12:38,417 --> 00:12:40,583
(ominous music swells)

237
00:12:44,208 --> 00:12:46,833
(Martha) <i>That night, she had her revenge.</i>

238
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
<i>In a demonic rage,</i>

239
00:12:52,083 --> 00:12:56,167
<i>- she defiled this holy place.</i>
- (grunts)

240
00:12:56,250 --> 00:12:58,625
(sinister music plays)

241
00:12:58,708 --> 00:13:00,083
(grunting)

242
00:13:00,167 --> 00:13:01,167
(screams)

243
00:13:01,917 --> 00:13:03,792
(grunts, yells)

244
00:13:07,042 --> 00:13:08,042
(grunts, yells)

245
00:13:11,417 --> 00:13:13,833
(Martha) <i>Blasphemy, desecration.</i>

246
00:13:15,417 --> 00:13:18,417
<i>- Evil incarnate.</i>
- (yells)

247
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:19,708
(music fades)

248
00:13:21,250 --> 00:13:23,292
(Grace breathing heavily)

249
00:13:27,458 --> 00:13:28,458
(squeals)

250
00:13:29,167 --> 00:13:30,167
(grunts)

251
00:13:34,417 --> 00:13:35,458
(breath trembling)

252
00:13:37,208 --> 00:13:39,250
(low unsettling music plays)

253
00:13:39,333 --> 00:13:41,333
(breathing heavily)

254
00:13:43,292 --> 00:13:44,292
(sniffles)

255
00:13:47,250 --> 00:13:49,250
(thunder rumbling)

256
00:13:54,083 --> 00:13:56,458
(Martha) <i>I said, "Sister Grace,</i>"

257
00:13:56,542 --> 00:14:01,458
<i>God, your Father,
will forgive you in His love."</i>

258
00:14:05,083 --> 00:14:06,083
(music fades)

259
00:14:07,542 --> 00:14:09,500
- (Martha screams)
- (yells)

260
00:14:09,583 --> 00:14:11,333
- (grunting)
- (yelping)

261
00:14:11,417 --> 00:14:13,417
(menacing music plays)

262
00:14:16,542 --> 00:14:17,958
(yelling)

263
00:14:18,042 --> 00:14:19,542
- (music stops)
- She died,

264
00:14:20,083 --> 00:14:22,375
throwing herself against Prentice's tomb.

265
00:14:22,458 --> 00:14:24,125
(screams)

266
00:14:25,542 --> 00:14:27,542
(Martha) <i>Brain aneurysm, they said.</i>

267
00:14:28,750 --> 00:14:30,667
Struck down, I say,

268
00:14:31,542 --> 00:14:34,875
by God in His mercy.

269
00:14:35,958 --> 00:14:37,500
Holy shit.

270
00:14:39,667 --> 00:14:40,667
Sorry, sorry.

271
00:14:41,667 --> 00:14:42,917
(intriguing music plays)

272
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:46,333
(Jud) <i>The darkness of that story</i>
<i>was the bedrock of this place.</i>

273
00:14:46,417 --> 00:14:48,667
- (Wicks speaks indistinctly)
- (Jud) <i>You could feel it.</i>

274
00:14:48,750 --> 00:14:50,083
And he asked, "Who told you…"

275
00:14:50,167 --> 00:14:54,500
(Jud) <i>So what would draw someone</i>
<i>to make this their spiritual home?</i>

276
00:14:55,083 --> 00:14:58,083
<i>The core group of regulars
all had their reasons.</i>

277
00:14:58,167 --> 00:15:00,125
<i>And these will be our suspects.</i>

278
00:15:00,208 --> 00:15:01,833
<i>So I should introduce them.</i>

279
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:06,208
<i>Vera Draven, local attorney.
She was loyal and devoted.</i>

280
00:15:06,292 --> 00:15:07,667
- (lock chirps)
- (whistle blows)

281
00:15:08,625 --> 00:15:11,958
(Vera) My dad was
Wicks' attorney and drinking buddy.

282
00:15:12,042 --> 00:15:14,167
(chuckles) The boys.

283
00:15:14,250 --> 00:15:18,625
And so you became a lawyer so you could
take over his family practice?

284
00:15:18,708 --> 00:15:21,792
I became a lawyer because
I wanted to do great things in the world.

285
00:15:23,125 --> 00:15:25,958
- But this place is my dad's legacy.
- (spoon clatters)

286
00:15:26,708 --> 00:15:27,750
And he wanted me to…

287
00:15:27,833 --> 00:15:30,208
- Thank you.
- …keep it going after he passed.

288
00:15:30,292 --> 00:15:32,708
So here I am.

289
00:15:32,792 --> 00:15:34,625
- Yo, yo. Hey, Vera.
- (door closes)

290
00:15:34,708 --> 00:15:37,042
- Hi. Oh. Thank you.
- Would you fill this up?

291
00:15:37,125 --> 00:15:39,625
(Jud) <i>Her adopted son, Cy,</i>
<i>just moved back home</i>

292
00:15:39,708 --> 00:15:42,417
<i>after trying and failing
to get a foothold in politics.</i>

293
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:43,417
Hi.

294
00:15:43,500 --> 00:15:44,500
Hey.

295
00:15:44,542 --> 00:15:45,625
Okay.

296
00:15:46,667 --> 00:15:48,208
It's nice having him back?

297
00:15:48,292 --> 00:15:50,375
(intriguing music plays)

298
00:15:51,417 --> 00:15:54,417
(Jud) <i>When Vera was young,</i>
<i>just out of law school,</i>

299
00:15:54,500 --> 00:15:58,250
<i>her dad came home
with ten-year-old Cy, out of the blue,</i>

300
00:15:58,333 --> 00:16:00,667
<i>and told Vera she was gonna raise him.</i>

301
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:02,167
<i>No questions asked.</i>

302
00:16:03,750 --> 00:16:07,417
<i>The whole town knew he was
obviously her illegitimate brother.</i>

303
00:16:08,083 --> 00:16:09,500
<i>But Vera accepted it.</i>

304
00:16:10,458 --> 00:16:11,750
<i>She did it.</i>

305
00:16:11,833 --> 00:16:14,417
I've given up a lot to… (inhales deeply)

306
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:19,042
…be loyal to my dad
and to Cy and to Wicks.

307
00:16:20,375 --> 00:16:21,708
So I think that

308
00:16:21,792 --> 00:16:25,208
when my dad
is looking down on me from heaven,

309
00:16:25,292 --> 00:16:28,708
I think he is very… (inhales deeply)

310
00:16:28,792 --> 00:16:30,500
…pleased with me.

311
00:16:32,875 --> 00:16:34,292
So I guess that's nice.

312
00:16:34,958 --> 00:16:36,042
(Cy) <i>I came this close.</i>

313
00:16:36,125 --> 00:16:38,667
I was the GOP golden boy, the great hope.

314
00:16:38,750 --> 00:16:41,292
I've got connections and ins and outs.

315
00:16:41,375 --> 00:16:43,500
I was on the cusp,
but I just couldn't engage voters.

316
00:16:43,583 --> 00:16:47,500
I didn't… (inhales) have that
cult of personality thing, I guess.

317
00:16:47,583 --> 00:16:50,417
It's hard connecting with people
in a genuine way.

318
00:16:50,500 --> 00:16:52,208
- I know.
- (Jud) Mm-hmm.

319
00:16:52,292 --> 00:16:54,708
I tried everything. Believe me,
I hammered the race thing.

320
00:16:54,792 --> 00:16:57,000
I hammered the gender thing,
the trans thing,

321
00:16:57,083 --> 00:16:59,208
the border thing, the homeless thing,

322
00:16:59,292 --> 00:17:03,125
the war thing, the election thing,
the abortion thing, the climate thing.

323
00:17:03,917 --> 00:17:07,917
Thing about induction stoves,
Israel, library books, vaccines,

324
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:11,083
pronouns, AK-47s, socialism, BLM,

325
00:17:11,167 --> 00:17:15,000
CRT, the CDC, DEI, 5G, everything.

326
00:17:15,708 --> 00:17:19,708
All of it I did.
Nobody, just nothing. (Sighs)

327
00:17:20,458 --> 00:17:22,667
People are just numb these days.
I don't know why.

328
00:17:22,750 --> 00:17:25,625
(hesitates) Maybe we need to get back
to fundamentals, you know,

329
00:17:25,708 --> 00:17:29,167
basic building blocks
on how to genuinely inspire people.

330
00:17:29,250 --> 00:17:31,292
The basics,
like, show them something they hate

331
00:17:31,375 --> 00:17:34,042
and then make them afraid it's gonna
take away something they love?

332
00:17:34,125 --> 00:17:35,750
(soft intriguing music plays)

333
00:17:35,833 --> 00:17:37,125
(hesitates) Well, no.

334
00:17:37,208 --> 00:17:38,542
- (indistinct chatter)
- Vera.

335
00:17:38,625 --> 00:17:41,333
- (Jud) <i>Nat Sharp, the town's local doctor.</i>
- (imperceptible)

336
00:17:41,417 --> 00:17:43,625
<i>His life revolved around his wife, Darla.</i>

337
00:17:44,292 --> 00:17:45,958
<i>She was his everything.</i>

338
00:17:46,042 --> 00:17:47,875
- (music stops)
- Darla left me last week.

339
00:17:47,958 --> 00:17:49,625
(tuts) Oh, I'm sorry.

340
00:17:50,125 --> 00:17:53,000
Took the kids, moved to Tucson

341
00:17:54,167 --> 00:17:56,792
with some guy that she met
on a Phish message board.

342
00:17:58,792 --> 00:17:59,792
(smacks lips)

343
00:18:00,417 --> 00:18:01,625
Phish the band?

344
00:18:01,708 --> 00:18:03,125
I had no idea.

345
00:18:03,958 --> 00:18:06,875
- (somber music plays)
- (Jud) <i>Dr. Nat was spinning out.</i>

346
00:18:06,958 --> 00:18:10,792
<i>He wasn't successful enough,
rich enough, good enough for her.</i>

347
00:18:10,875 --> 00:18:13,000
<i>He would do anything to get her back.</i>

348
00:18:15,875 --> 00:18:17,125
(intriguing music plays)

349
00:18:17,208 --> 00:18:19,833
<i>The closest thing we have
to a local celebrity,</i>

350
00:18:19,917 --> 00:18:22,042
<i>the sci-fi writer Lee Ross.</i>

351
00:18:23,125 --> 00:18:25,042
<i>You probably know
at least some of his books.</i>

352
00:18:25,667 --> 00:18:28,083
The Crescent Limbo <i>series,</i>
Ice Pick of Time,

353
00:18:28,167 --> 00:18:29,500
The Crystaline Juncture.

354
00:18:30,292 --> 00:18:32,792
<i>Ten years ago,
Lee moved here from New York,</i>

355
00:18:32,875 --> 00:18:35,875
<i>connected with Wicks and the church,
and as he puts it…</i>

356
00:18:35,958 --> 00:18:37,458
Unplugged my brain, you know,

357
00:18:37,542 --> 00:18:41,958
from the… (inhales) liberal hive mind
and… (exhales) come here and…

358
00:18:42,875 --> 00:18:46,833
(Jud) <i>His book sales and popular standing</i>
<i>have been in slow decline ever since.</i>

359
00:18:47,958 --> 00:18:51,375
<i>But he'd spent the last year
writing a massive book about Wicks.</i>

360
00:18:51,458 --> 00:18:52,958
His teachings, my reflections,

361
00:18:53,042 --> 00:18:56,458
essays and recollections
of an acolyte at the feet of a prophet.

362
00:18:56,542 --> 00:18:58,708
(Jud) The Holy Man and the Troubadour.

363
00:18:58,792 --> 00:19:02,417
<i>I found it a tough read,
but he pinned all of his hopes on it.</i>

364
00:19:02,500 --> 00:19:05,875
This is my last chance ticket
out of Substack hell.

365
00:19:05,958 --> 00:19:08,625
I can't take it anymore.
My readers these days…

366
00:19:09,125 --> 00:19:11,542
I mean, they are survivalist freaks.

367
00:19:11,625 --> 00:19:14,250
They all look like John Goodman
in <i>The Big Lebowski.</i>

368
00:19:14,333 --> 00:19:15,583
(chuckles)

369
00:19:16,458 --> 00:19:18,125
(church bell tolling)

370
00:19:18,208 --> 00:19:21,292
This little shitwick, Cy.

371
00:19:22,042 --> 00:19:25,250
He's got his little influencer fangs
in the Monsignor lately.

372
00:19:25,333 --> 00:19:28,000
We don't like him. We're all like,
"Wicks. Be careful,

373
00:19:28,083 --> 00:19:32,167
you gotta shake him off.
He's bad news. Opportunistic poetaster."

374
00:19:33,167 --> 00:19:36,792
We're gonna have to protect Wicks
from these leeching millennials.

375
00:19:36,875 --> 00:19:39,667
(Jud) <i>Simone was new to town,</i>
<i>and new to the church.</i>

376
00:19:39,750 --> 00:19:43,292
<i>- She'd been a world-class cellist.</i>
- (playing dramatic, somber tune)

377
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:49,583
<i>Forced to retire five years ago
for health reasons.</i>

378
00:19:49,667 --> 00:19:52,500
<i>Chronic pain. Some mysterious nerve thing</i>

379
00:19:52,583 --> 00:19:54,833
<i>- that doctors cannot diagnose.</i>
- Sorry. (Panting)

380
00:19:54,917 --> 00:19:57,083
I believed they could heal me.

381
00:19:57,167 --> 00:19:58,792
(clicks tongue) Suckered.

382
00:19:59,292 --> 00:20:00,708
Dipshit <i>moi.</i>

383
00:20:00,792 --> 00:20:02,167
(breathes deeply)

384
00:20:02,250 --> 00:20:04,500
I mean, to take someone's faith

385
00:20:04,583 --> 00:20:06,208
and exploit it for money

386
00:20:08,083 --> 00:20:09,500
is the ultimate evil.

387
00:20:11,750 --> 00:20:13,167
Don't you think it is?

388
00:20:13,250 --> 00:20:15,375
Yeah. Yeah, it's bad.

389
00:20:16,792 --> 00:20:18,917
But I understand wanting to believe.

390
00:20:20,958 --> 00:20:22,583
This feels different, though.

391
00:20:22,667 --> 00:20:24,333
(intriguing music plays)

392
00:20:24,417 --> 00:20:26,833
Faith in God to heal me.

393
00:20:29,042 --> 00:20:30,250
This is different.

394
00:20:33,625 --> 00:20:36,250
I feel hopeful now.

395
00:20:38,125 --> 00:20:40,417
Like a miracle could happen.

396
00:20:43,500 --> 00:20:45,917
That's how Monsignor Wicks makes me feel.

397
00:20:49,750 --> 00:20:52,125
- (intriguing music continues)
- (wind gusting)

398
00:20:55,042 --> 00:20:56,708
(indistinct chatter)

399
00:20:56,792 --> 00:20:59,708
- The Spirit really moved him today, huh?
- (gasps)

400
00:21:00,958 --> 00:21:02,167
Monsignor!

401
00:21:03,083 --> 00:21:04,333
Ah, my warrior.

402
00:21:04,917 --> 00:21:08,000
- (Jud) <i>Wicks kept his core group tight.</i>
- (laughs)

403
00:21:08,083 --> 00:21:11,583
(Jud) <i>And the seductive power</i>
<i>of his charisma was undeniable.</i>

404
00:21:13,667 --> 00:21:14,875
<i>But his method…</i>

405
00:21:15,875 --> 00:21:17,917
<i>Every week, he would pick someone out,</i>

406
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,958
<i>a newcomer usually, and he would attack.</i>

407
00:21:21,042 --> 00:21:24,125
The world wants us all to be okay.

408
00:21:25,958 --> 00:21:29,792
Any of your choices.
Make your choices. They're your choices.

409
00:21:30,292 --> 00:21:31,500
Don't feel bad.

410
00:21:32,208 --> 00:21:33,500
Have that affair.

411
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:35,208
Tell that lie.

412
00:21:35,292 --> 00:21:37,625
Have that child out of wedlock.

413
00:21:37,708 --> 00:21:40,500
Satisfy your selfish heart.

414
00:21:41,125 --> 00:21:42,542
Selfish.

415
00:21:42,625 --> 00:21:43,667
Yes.

416
00:21:44,208 --> 00:21:47,333
Depriving that child of a family.

417
00:21:48,250 --> 00:21:49,583
Of a father.

418
00:21:50,083 --> 00:21:52,292
An assault on our castle.

419
00:21:52,375 --> 00:21:54,375
The institution of manhood.

420
00:21:54,458 --> 00:21:56,917
My own mother made
that selfish choice with me.

421
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,125
And I curse her selfish heart for it.

422
00:21:59,208 --> 00:22:01,625
Every day of my life.

423
00:22:01,708 --> 00:22:05,875
Putting her needs and wants
before the family God intended.

424
00:22:05,958 --> 00:22:07,167
I am enough!

425
00:22:07,750 --> 00:22:10,417
- Me!
- (tense music plays)

426
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:14,417
Selfish, harlot heart, you are not.

427
00:22:15,208 --> 00:22:16,708
Might as well beat that child.

428
00:22:16,792 --> 00:22:19,458
Yes, might as well starve that child.

429
00:22:19,542 --> 00:22:22,375
Defy the family that the Lord intended.

430
00:22:22,458 --> 00:22:26,292
And watch your child
burn beneath that burden.

431
00:22:27,208 --> 00:22:29,125
- (music subsides)
- (imperceptible)

432
00:22:29,208 --> 00:22:31,333
(Jud) <i>This is not the true church.</i>

433
00:22:32,292 --> 00:22:35,375
<i>You ask even the most hardcore
of those in the pews,</i>

434
00:22:35,458 --> 00:22:38,125
<i>they'll say, no,
of course this is not what they believe.</i>

435
00:22:38,208 --> 00:22:40,958
<i>It's Wicks being Wicks,
pushing it too far.</i>

436
00:22:41,042 --> 00:22:44,958
<i>And what he's pushing for
every time is a walkout.</i>

437
00:22:45,042 --> 00:22:46,375
(intriguing music plays)

438
00:22:46,458 --> 00:22:47,875
<i>Why does he do this?</i>

439
00:22:48,458 --> 00:22:51,458
<i>Because when that person walks out,
everyone watches.</i>

440
00:22:52,042 --> 00:22:54,792
<i>And even if in the light of day
it's indefensible,</i>

441
00:22:54,875 --> 00:22:58,042
<i>deep down in the dark,
it scratches an itch.</i>

442
00:22:58,125 --> 00:22:59,917
(imperceptible)

443
00:23:02,250 --> 00:23:05,667
(Jud) <i>And by staying put in that pew,</i>
<i>a side is taken.</i>

444
00:23:06,917 --> 00:23:08,625
<i>Wicks' side.</i>

445
00:23:08,708 --> 00:23:11,708
<i>Testing tolerances,
tapping deep poisoned wells,</i>

446
00:23:11,792 --> 00:23:13,792
<i>hardening, binding with complicity.</i>

447
00:23:13,875 --> 00:23:17,333
(man over radio) <i>…for a two-run homer</i>
<i>and that brings the Cubs…</i>

448
00:23:17,417 --> 00:23:18,917
(grunts) Mm.

449
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,583
- (Jud) <i>So I try to offer a counterbalance.</i>
- (music fades)

450
00:23:22,667 --> 00:23:26,042
Okay, welcome to our first
Father Jud prayer group.

451
00:23:26,125 --> 00:23:28,583
Um, thank you all for being here.

452
00:23:29,250 --> 00:23:32,042
And this is all about breaking down walls

453
00:23:32,125 --> 00:23:34,542
between us and Christ,

454
00:23:35,250 --> 00:23:38,083
us and each other, us and the world.

455
00:23:39,333 --> 00:23:40,958
When I was 17,

456
00:23:41,458 --> 00:23:43,000
I was a boxer

457
00:23:43,083 --> 00:23:45,583
and I… I… killed a man in the ring.

458
00:23:47,458 --> 00:23:49,375
I built up so many walls of…

459
00:23:50,042 --> 00:23:52,833
Of anger, addiction, violence.

460
00:23:54,333 --> 00:23:56,542
It was only when I felt safe enough to…

461
00:23:57,125 --> 00:24:00,958
To put my dukes down, open my arms,

462
00:24:01,042 --> 00:24:03,125
um, confess my deepest sin.

463
00:24:03,792 --> 00:24:06,208
That was the day
that Christ saved my life.

464
00:24:06,958 --> 00:24:09,583
It… He didn't… He didn't transform me.

465
00:24:09,667 --> 00:24:12,375
He sustains me every day.

466
00:24:12,458 --> 00:24:13,875
(clock ticking)

467
00:24:13,958 --> 00:24:15,458
That's daily bread, right?

468
00:24:16,958 --> 00:24:19,083
I think that's what the church should be.

469
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,417
That's what I want
this church to be, for me

470
00:24:23,083 --> 00:24:24,500
and for all of you.

471
00:24:29,167 --> 00:24:30,458
- (Vera chuckles softly)
- So…

472
00:24:33,625 --> 00:24:36,042
Monsignor Wicks isn't coming?

473
00:24:38,042 --> 00:24:39,667
(hesitates) No, but…

474
00:24:40,458 --> 00:24:41,458
No.

475
00:24:41,542 --> 00:24:44,625
So, I thought we could just talk
and start sharing and...

476
00:24:44,708 --> 00:24:46,333
But he knows that we're doing these?

477
00:24:46,417 --> 00:24:48,583
Sure, of course. Of course, I'll tell him.

478
00:24:48,667 --> 00:24:50,833
I just… I wanted us to connect and, uh…

479
00:24:50,917 --> 00:24:53,125
You'll tell him? You'll… You'll tell him?

480
00:24:53,208 --> 00:24:56,625
Contraction of the simple future tense
meaning you haven't yet

481
00:24:57,250 --> 00:24:58,458
told him?

482
00:24:59,417 --> 00:25:00,833
Why wouldn't you tell him?

483
00:25:00,917 --> 00:25:02,875
- This feels kind of weird.
- (Jud) Okay, guys.

484
00:25:03,458 --> 00:25:06,542
(chuckles) This is a prayer meeting.
It's… it's not a secret or anything.

485
00:25:06,625 --> 00:25:08,792
- Well, it's a secret prayer meeting.
- No.

486
00:25:08,875 --> 00:25:10,583
That's literally what it is.

487
00:25:10,667 --> 00:25:11,833
I just texted the Monsignor.

488
00:25:11,917 --> 00:25:14,333
Great, so… (chuckles)
it isn't a secret anymore.

489
00:25:14,417 --> 00:25:17,625
Now, if we could get back to breaking
down those walls through Christ's love...

490
00:25:17,708 --> 00:25:18,708
(cell phone chimes)

491
00:25:18,750 --> 00:25:21,167
- He says, "What the holy heck?"
- Oh.

492
00:25:21,250 --> 00:25:23,458
- Jeez.
- (Vera) I'm… I'm sorry. Father,

493
00:25:23,542 --> 00:25:27,542
I came because I thought
this was an official church function.

494
00:25:27,625 --> 00:25:29,833
Only he didn't use the word "heck".

495
00:25:29,917 --> 00:25:31,458
- (laughs)
- Okay, thank you, Martha.

496
00:25:31,542 --> 00:25:32,875
This is a church function, Vera.

497
00:25:32,958 --> 00:25:34,792
It's at a church.
It's with me. It's official.

498
00:25:34,875 --> 00:25:36,167
- Um…
- Or "holy".

499
00:25:36,250 --> 00:25:38,458
- Show… Can I see that?
- I think I should go.

500
00:25:38,542 --> 00:25:39,542
- (Nat) Okay.
- (Lee) Bye.

501
00:25:39,625 --> 00:25:41,500
- (Vera) Thank you. Uh, Cyrus?
- Yeah.

502
00:25:41,583 --> 00:25:42,458
(Nat) Oh, wow.

503
00:25:42,542 --> 00:25:44,875
Yeah, sorry, Father.
I don't want to piss him off.

504
00:25:45,583 --> 00:25:47,083
Nice try, guy.

505
00:25:47,167 --> 00:25:48,167
Thanks, Doc.

506
00:25:48,208 --> 00:25:50,625
I'm probably gonna post this tomorrow.
Can I tag you?

507
00:25:50,708 --> 00:25:51,875
I'd prefer you didn't.

508
00:25:51,958 --> 00:25:53,042
I probably will anyway.

509
00:25:53,125 --> 00:25:54,167
- I know.
- (door opens)

510
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:56,000
(door closes)

511
00:25:56,625 --> 00:26:00,375
I'm so sorry your little coup failed
this afternoon, Father.

512
00:26:00,458 --> 00:26:02,042
My coup? Martha, really?

513
00:26:02,125 --> 00:26:05,542
If we want to pray
or need to confess something,

514
00:26:05,625 --> 00:26:08,000
we can do it with Monsignor Wicks.

515
00:26:08,083 --> 00:26:10,917
Can you? 'Cause you all seem
scared to death of the guy.

516
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Could you walk into that church
of your own free will

517
00:26:14,083 --> 00:26:17,292
and confess your deepest sin to Wicks,
Martha, without fear?

518
00:26:17,375 --> 00:26:20,750
'Cause… 'cause if not,
this whole place is a whitewashed tomb.

519
00:26:20,833 --> 00:26:23,250
Yes, I could.

520
00:26:23,333 --> 00:26:24,417
Well,

521
00:26:25,625 --> 00:26:27,042
good!

522
00:26:30,042 --> 00:26:32,042
(church bell tolling)

523
00:26:40,667 --> 00:26:41,667
(scoffs)

524
00:26:48,458 --> 00:26:49,708
(groans)

525
00:26:49,792 --> 00:26:52,250
(panting)

526
00:26:56,917 --> 00:26:58,417
(exhales sharply)

527
00:26:59,583 --> 00:27:03,417
(Jud) <i>Holy Week, the week</i>
<i>of special services leading up to Easter.</i>

528
00:27:04,375 --> 00:27:07,208
<i>It was on Palm Sunday
that I finally broke.</i>

529
00:27:07,292 --> 00:27:10,500
(Wicks) <i>And then, uh…</i>
<i>twice in the shower this week</i>

530
00:27:11,333 --> 00:27:14,792
doing that thing I told you about
where I hold my hand upside down?

531
00:27:16,042 --> 00:27:18,458
Five Our Fathers, five Hail Marys.

532
00:27:19,792 --> 00:27:22,250
Well, we're at nine months now, Jud.

533
00:27:23,792 --> 00:27:28,583
How are you enjoying it here?
Are you breaking down some… walls?

534
00:27:32,250 --> 00:27:36,125
Bless me, Father, for I have sinned.
It's been a week since my last confession.

535
00:27:38,917 --> 00:27:40,125
(inhales sharply)

536
00:27:40,208 --> 00:27:42,833
I betrayed a fellow priest's privacy.

537
00:27:44,042 --> 00:27:47,458
I know that Martha keeps
her medical bills filed in the office, so…

538
00:27:48,417 --> 00:27:49,625
I went through them.

539
00:27:50,667 --> 00:27:53,375
And I learned you had
a radical prostatectomy five years ago,

540
00:27:53,458 --> 00:27:56,708
making you physically incapable
of an erection.

541
00:27:59,167 --> 00:28:01,583
I can handle whatever this is.

542
00:28:02,125 --> 00:28:05,708
For the past nine months,
I've seen the way you tend this flock,

543
00:28:05,792 --> 00:28:07,125
and I don't like it.

544
00:28:07,208 --> 00:28:08,292
You don't like it?

545
00:28:08,375 --> 00:28:10,208
No, Monsignor, I don't.

546
00:28:10,292 --> 00:28:11,500
Nat Sharp.

547
00:28:11,583 --> 00:28:14,792
Man needs to forgive
and start living his life.

548
00:28:14,875 --> 00:28:17,792
And Christ's love should be
a launching pad for that, and instead,

549
00:28:17,875 --> 00:28:19,583
he's just, every day,

550
00:28:19,667 --> 00:28:22,875
getting more and more angry
and bitter against his ex-wife,

551
00:28:22,958 --> 00:28:23,958
<i>- against women.</i>
- (grunts)

552
00:28:24,917 --> 00:28:28,250
It's bad. And Lee, he's a storyteller.

553
00:28:28,333 --> 00:28:31,833
And it's like his superpower
has been turned against him.

554
00:28:31,917 --> 00:28:34,792
The only story he tells is,
"The world is out to get me."

555
00:28:34,875 --> 00:28:38,125
He was brilliant funny,
and smart and respected,

556
00:28:38,208 --> 00:28:43,792
and now he's just spinning out
and angry all the time, paranoid.

557
00:28:43,875 --> 00:28:46,917
Did you know he literally
built a moat around his house?

558
00:28:47,500 --> 00:28:48,500
Really?

559
00:28:49,500 --> 00:28:52,333
(Jud) <i>I mean,</i>
<i>it's mostly symbolic, but yeah.</i>

560
00:28:53,125 --> 00:28:55,125
And Simone, I'm sorry, Monsignor,

561
00:28:55,208 --> 00:28:57,417
but I'm afraid
you've taken advantage of her.

562
00:28:57,500 --> 00:28:59,333
I've seen the donation numbers.

563
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,125
I know how much she's giving,
and the past few months,

564
00:29:02,208 --> 00:29:03,917
she's basically supporting this place.

565
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:07,708
<i>And yes, I believe in the possibility
of miracles through Christ,</i>

566
00:29:07,792 --> 00:29:09,792
<i>but what you're giving her is not that.</i>

567
00:29:11,000 --> 00:29:13,208
It's transactional, through you,

568
00:29:13,292 --> 00:29:15,625
and God help you,
it makes her feel betrayed again.

569
00:29:16,208 --> 00:29:17,875
God help me. Anything else?

570
00:29:17,958 --> 00:29:20,208
Yeah, why not? Cy Draven.

571
00:29:20,292 --> 00:29:23,667
<i>Doesn't this new YouTube stuff
he's doing worry you?</i>

572
00:29:23,750 --> 00:29:26,167
<i>Every week now, he takes clips
from what you say in Mass</i>

573
00:29:26,250 --> 00:29:30,750
<i>and plugs them into his political rants.
He's co-opting, and honestly,</i>

574
00:29:30,833 --> 00:29:33,417
<i>I think misrepresenting the church
in a dangerous way.</i>

575
00:29:33,500 --> 00:29:34,583
(sighs)

576
00:29:34,667 --> 00:29:38,333
When was the last time
a new person lasted more than one Sunday?

577
00:29:38,958 --> 00:29:40,333
Word's gotten out.

578
00:29:40,917 --> 00:29:42,917
Every week now, it's just this…

579
00:29:43,000 --> 00:29:45,000
This hardened cyst of regulars,

580
00:29:45,083 --> 00:29:49,375
and it seems like you're intentionally
keeping them angry and afraid.

581
00:29:50,333 --> 00:29:52,042
Is that how Christ led his flock?

582
00:29:52,125 --> 00:29:54,250
Is that what we're supposed to do?

583
00:29:59,250 --> 00:30:00,667
- (grunts)
- (groans)

584
00:30:00,750 --> 00:30:02,708
- (crows cawing, fluttering)
- (coughing)

585
00:30:02,792 --> 00:30:06,458
Right now, you're angry. You should be.

586
00:30:06,542 --> 00:30:08,875
It'd be dangerous if you weren't.
I'd see you're helpless,

587
00:30:08,958 --> 00:30:11,000
and I'd do it again and again.

588
00:30:11,792 --> 00:30:14,167
I'm the world. You're the church.

589
00:30:14,250 --> 00:30:17,125
- Stay there. Stay there. Stay.
- (groaning)

590
00:30:18,500 --> 00:30:21,958
- Aha. Good. Right. Yes.
- (pants, grunts)

591
00:30:22,542 --> 00:30:26,625
Anger. Anger lets us fight back,
take back the ground we've lost.

592
00:30:27,208 --> 00:30:29,375
And we've lost so much ground.

593
00:30:29,958 --> 00:30:31,083
And now you're afraid.

594
00:30:31,167 --> 00:30:34,042
Look at those bare-knuckle instincts
coming back. Good!

595
00:30:34,875 --> 00:30:37,333
You're afraid I'm gonna come at you again.

596
00:30:37,417 --> 00:30:40,750
- But you're protecting yourself.
- (pants, exhales)

597
00:30:43,125 --> 00:30:46,542
Because the world wants to destroy us.

598
00:30:47,500 --> 00:30:51,333
Your version of love
and forgiveness is a sop.

599
00:30:51,417 --> 00:30:54,417
It's going along
to get along with modernity,

600
00:30:54,500 --> 00:30:56,708
not wanting to offend this garbage world.

601
00:30:56,792 --> 00:31:00,042
Meanwhile, they destroy us.

602
00:31:00,708 --> 00:31:05,125
Feminist Marxist whores.
Bit by bit, they do.

603
00:31:07,208 --> 00:31:10,417
But I carry my burden. I hold the line.

604
00:31:12,458 --> 00:31:13,542
And you?

605
00:31:14,750 --> 00:31:17,167
You simpering child from Albany?

606
00:31:19,667 --> 00:31:21,292
Are you gonna get angry

607
00:31:22,375 --> 00:31:23,750
and fight?

608
00:31:32,083 --> 00:31:34,083
You're poisoning this church.

609
00:31:35,250 --> 00:31:37,667
And I'll do whatever it takes to save it.

610
00:31:39,458 --> 00:31:41,292
To cut you out like a cancer.

611
00:31:44,500 --> 00:31:46,917
Five Our Fathers, five Hail Marys.

612
00:32:02,667 --> 00:32:03,667
(door opens)

613
00:32:07,583 --> 00:32:09,583
(Jud) <i>Christ, you didn't give up on me.</i>

614
00:32:10,167 --> 00:32:12,417
<i>I'm not giving up on this church.</i>

615
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:14,833
(thunder rumbling)

616
00:32:14,917 --> 00:32:16,833
<i>But he was one step ahead of me.</i>

617
00:32:17,667 --> 00:32:20,083
(Wicks) Father Jud's prayer meeting?

618
00:32:20,792 --> 00:32:22,292
I have kept this church.

619
00:32:22,375 --> 00:32:24,083
I have fortified it with the truth of God.

620
00:32:24,167 --> 00:32:27,458
And now the betrayal to find my authority

621
00:32:27,542 --> 00:32:30,667
and faith and life itself challenged,

622
00:32:30,750 --> 00:32:32,833
and from inside my own sanctuary.

623
00:32:35,333 --> 00:32:36,417
Get out!

624
00:32:37,875 --> 00:32:38,708
(door slams)

625
00:32:38,792 --> 00:32:40,083
(breathing heavily)

626
00:32:40,167 --> 00:32:41,792
(Jud) <i>Wicks' final move.</i>

627
00:32:41,875 --> 00:32:43,708
<i>Open war against me.</i>

628
00:32:43,792 --> 00:32:46,417
- (rock music plays over speakers)
- (lively chatter)

629
00:32:46,500 --> 00:32:48,167
(Jud) <i>I got pretty toasty.</i>

630
00:32:48,750 --> 00:32:51,583
The world's a wolf. You devil.

631
00:32:51,667 --> 00:32:53,292
You devil wolf.

632
00:32:53,792 --> 00:32:55,208
You're a devil wolf.

633
00:32:56,000 --> 00:32:57,208
Shit.

634
00:32:57,708 --> 00:33:01,250
- Oh! Nikolai, I'm sorry. I broke that…
- No, no, no. Don't worry about it.

635
00:33:01,333 --> 00:33:03,750
- …with my hand.
- They're junk lamps. They're junk lamps.

636
00:33:03,833 --> 00:33:05,583
- Come on. Hey, are you good to drive?
- Yeah.

637
00:33:05,667 --> 00:33:06,500
Are you sure?

638
00:33:06,583 --> 00:33:08,583
(suspenseful music plays)

639
00:33:20,958 --> 00:33:22,625
- (grunts)
- (glass shatters)

640
00:33:22,708 --> 00:33:23,833
- (Sam) Hey!
- (gasps)

641
00:33:26,625 --> 00:33:27,833
Who's there?

642
00:33:27,917 --> 00:33:28,917
Oh, shit.

643
00:33:30,333 --> 00:33:32,458
(Jud) <i>And that brings us to Good Friday.</i>

644
00:33:34,625 --> 00:33:36,000
<i>Here we go.</i>

645
00:33:36,083 --> 00:33:38,208
<i>It was a 3:00 p.m. service.</i>

646
00:33:39,125 --> 00:33:40,375
<i>Just the regulars.</i>

647
00:33:41,208 --> 00:33:43,000
<i>There was a strange tension in the air.</i>

648
00:33:43,708 --> 00:33:46,000
<i>I can't recall the homily,
but it felt different.</i>

649
00:33:47,500 --> 00:33:49,875
<i>The anger felt less calculated.</i>

650
00:33:50,875 --> 00:33:51,792
<i>More unhinged.</i>

651
00:33:51,875 --> 00:33:54,833
- (imperceptible)
- (suspenseful music continues)

652
00:33:57,708 --> 00:34:01,375
(Jud) <i>As always, after the homily,</i>
<i>Wicks was spent,</i>

653
00:34:01,458 --> 00:34:04,667
<i>emotionally and physically,
and needed some time to recover.</i>

654
00:34:06,708 --> 00:34:09,042
<i>He would duck into
the small storage closet</i>

655
00:34:09,125 --> 00:34:12,000
<i>just to the side of the sanctuary
so he'd be out of sight.</i>

656
00:34:13,208 --> 00:34:16,292
<i>He'd fortify himself,
and I would continue the service</i>

657
00:34:16,375 --> 00:34:19,542
<i>until he felt strong enough
to rejoin and take over.</i>

658
00:34:19,625 --> 00:34:22,458
<i>♪ Behold the wood of the cross ♪</i>

659
00:34:22,542 --> 00:34:25,625
<i>♪ On which hung
The salvation of the world ♪</i>

660
00:34:25,708 --> 00:34:27,125
- (thudding)
- (object clangs)

661
00:34:34,458 --> 00:34:35,708
(Jud) Monsignor?

662
00:34:35,792 --> 00:34:38,208
(low suspenseful music plays)

663
00:34:50,167 --> 00:34:51,250
What's wrong?

664
00:34:53,083 --> 00:34:54,125
Jefferson?

665
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
(suspenseful music rises)

666
00:35:16,583 --> 00:35:17,583
Jud.

667
00:35:20,625 --> 00:35:21,833
There's something in his back.

668
00:35:22,417 --> 00:35:23,583
(Nat) Wait. Don't touch it.

669
00:35:24,500 --> 00:35:25,917
Don't… don't touch anything.

670
00:35:36,875 --> 00:35:38,708
(music intensifies)

671
00:35:38,792 --> 00:35:39,958
(screaming)

672
00:35:44,458 --> 00:35:46,583
Satan! Satan struck him down!

673
00:35:46,667 --> 00:35:48,167
- The devil… Satan…
- (Vera) Martha.

674
00:35:48,250 --> 00:35:49,833
- …struck him down!
- Oh, no.

675
00:35:49,917 --> 00:35:52,708
- (Martha screaming)
- (Vera) Martha! Please.

676
00:35:57,208 --> 00:35:59,208
(sinister dramatic music plays)

677
00:36:12,458 --> 00:36:13,458
(music stops)

678
00:36:16,250 --> 00:36:18,292
(fire crackling)

679
00:36:19,792 --> 00:36:21,417
(thunder rumbles in distance)

680
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:32,083
(Jud) <i>The ambulance took</i>
<i>just five minutes to arrive.</i>

681
00:36:32,167 --> 00:36:34,375
- (sirens chirp)
- (sirens wailing)

682
00:36:34,458 --> 00:36:36,583
(Jud) <i>Wicks was pronounced dead</i>
<i>on the scene.</i>

683
00:36:37,292 --> 00:36:40,917
<i>When I joined the others outside,
the police were just arriving.</i>

684
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:43,833
(Martha) The devil will not take
that man. (Sniffles)

685
00:36:43,917 --> 00:36:46,750
He will rise again
in the glory of the Lord.

686
00:36:47,750 --> 00:36:50,583
(Jud) <i>This was an insane event</i>
<i>for a tiny town,</i>

687
00:36:50,667 --> 00:36:54,667
<i>and poor Geraldine, the local chief,
was thrown in headlong.</i>

688
00:36:54,750 --> 00:36:56,167
Christ.

689
00:36:57,375 --> 00:37:00,583
<i>Hours of questioning
all through the day Saturday</i>

690
00:37:01,250 --> 00:37:03,958
<i>- until finally…</i>
- (Geraldine) You're the only one on stage

691
00:37:04,042 --> 00:37:06,292
with the Monsignor
at the time of his killing.

692
00:37:07,042 --> 00:37:09,167
And you had prior possession
of the wolf-head figurine

693
00:37:09,250 --> 00:37:10,667
attached to the murder weapon.

694
00:37:12,500 --> 00:37:14,958
And you're the only one
at that church who hated his guts.

695
00:37:15,042 --> 00:37:16,875
I… I don't hate any guts.

696
00:37:16,958 --> 00:37:22,167
But it's literally impossible for anyone
to have done this, so I don't…

697
00:37:23,750 --> 00:37:26,833
(sighing) Oh, I don't know what this is.

698
00:37:31,250 --> 00:37:34,250
All right. Father,
why don't you go get some rest?

699
00:37:34,333 --> 00:37:37,208
But I should warn you before
you walk out of here, the town is talking.

700
00:37:37,833 --> 00:37:40,333
Cy Draven put this up
on his YouTube this morning.

701
00:37:40,417 --> 00:37:42,542
(Jud on recording) <i>…poisoning this church.</i>

702
00:37:42,625 --> 00:37:44,750
<i>And I'll do whatever it takes to save it.</i>

703
00:37:45,292 --> 00:37:49,125
<i>To cut you out like a cancer.</i>

704
00:37:50,958 --> 00:37:53,333
- (exhales)
- (Geraldine) It's been reposted.

705
00:37:54,833 --> 00:37:56,042
Quite a bit.

706
00:37:56,125 --> 00:37:58,125
(intriguing music plays)

707
00:37:59,083 --> 00:38:00,958
(indistinct police radio chatter)

708
00:38:03,167 --> 00:38:04,667
(object crunches)

709
00:38:09,417 --> 00:38:11,417
(music turns suspenseful)

710
00:38:31,208 --> 00:38:35,625
(Jud) <i>I turned on my phone</i>
<i>for two minutes, and that was a mistake.</i>

711
00:38:36,167 --> 00:38:39,667
<i>A flood of messages to the killer priest.</i>

712
00:38:41,875 --> 00:38:45,542
<i>But I wasn't thinking about
getting arrested or defrocked.</i>

713
00:38:46,083 --> 00:38:49,917
<i>I was thinking Wicks had won.</i>

714
00:38:51,125 --> 00:38:54,042
<i>Because in the part of my soul
that cannot lie to Christ,</i>

715
00:38:54,125 --> 00:38:56,958
<i>or myself, or you…</i>

716
00:38:57,042 --> 00:38:58,333
(door creaking)

717
00:38:58,417 --> 00:38:59,625
(door thuds)

718
00:39:00,333 --> 00:39:03,417
<i>…I was happy the old man was dead.</i>

719
00:39:03,500 --> 00:39:05,333
(solemn music plays)

720
00:39:07,625 --> 00:39:08,708
(exhales)

721
00:39:11,792 --> 00:39:12,875
Jesus.

722
00:39:13,708 --> 00:39:16,292
Help me. (Sniffles)

723
00:39:17,333 --> 00:39:19,750
Show me the way through this, please.

724
00:39:23,208 --> 00:39:24,083
(music fades)

725
00:39:24,167 --> 00:39:25,667
(man) Hello?

726
00:39:29,417 --> 00:39:31,042
Oh, I'm sorry. Um…

727
00:39:32,500 --> 00:39:33,917
Are you open?

728
00:39:35,042 --> 00:39:36,125
Always.

729
00:39:36,708 --> 00:39:37,917
You all right?

730
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:41,500
Yeah. Uh-huh. Sorry. (Sniffles)

731
00:39:42,875 --> 00:39:47,792
- Uh, there's no Easter Mass. I'm sorry.
- Oh, I'm…

732
00:39:47,875 --> 00:39:51,542
- You're welcome. Come in. Come in.
- Thank you. Thank you.

733
00:39:51,625 --> 00:39:56,042
I don't want to take you away
from your priestly duties now, do I?

734
00:39:56,833 --> 00:39:57,833
(Benoit clicks tongue)

735
00:39:58,417 --> 00:40:01,167
Well… (chuckles) Isn't this something?

736
00:40:01,250 --> 00:40:02,083
(Jud) Right?

737
00:40:02,167 --> 00:40:06,375
It's… hard to be in here
and not feel His presence.

738
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:08,083
Whose?

739
00:40:09,042 --> 00:40:12,083
Oh. God… Oh. Yeah. (Laughs)

740
00:40:12,167 --> 00:40:13,750
- Yeah.
- You're not a Catholic.

741
00:40:13,833 --> 00:40:16,000
No, very much not, no. (Chuckles)

742
00:40:16,875 --> 00:40:18,292
Proud heretic.

743
00:40:19,250 --> 00:40:21,458
I kneel at the altar of the rational.

744
00:40:21,542 --> 00:40:24,500
Uh-huh. You weren't raised in the faith?

745
00:40:25,250 --> 00:40:27,958
My mother is… was, uh,

746
00:40:28,042 --> 00:40:30,875
very, very religious, you know.

747
00:40:32,583 --> 00:40:34,417
- Were you close with her?
- No.

748
00:40:35,250 --> 00:40:39,125
When I was a boy, we, um… But it's…

749
00:40:39,208 --> 00:40:41,208
- Complicated. Family.
- (chuckles) It's complicated.

750
00:40:41,292 --> 00:40:42,708
Yeah. That's right.

751
00:40:47,792 --> 00:40:49,500
How does all this make you feel?

752
00:40:52,792 --> 00:40:54,625
How does it make me feel?

753
00:40:59,208 --> 00:41:00,042
Truthfully?

754
00:41:00,125 --> 00:41:01,208
Sure.

755
00:41:03,542 --> 00:41:05,375
Well, the architecture, that interests me.

756
00:41:06,375 --> 00:41:09,583
I feel the grandeur, the… the mystery,

757
00:41:09,667 --> 00:41:13,375
the intended emotional effect.
It's… (exhales)

758
00:41:15,750 --> 00:41:20,958
And it's like someone has shone
a story at me that I do not believe.

759
00:41:21,833 --> 00:41:25,500
It's built upon the empty promise
of a child's fairy tale

760
00:41:25,583 --> 00:41:30,250
filled with malevolence
and misogyny and homophobia

761
00:41:30,333 --> 00:41:34,500
and its justified
untold acts of violence and cruelty

762
00:41:34,583 --> 00:41:36,583
while all the while, and still,

763
00:41:36,667 --> 00:41:39,500
hiding its own shameful acts.

764
00:41:39,583 --> 00:41:41,708
So like an ornery mule kicking back,

765
00:41:41,792 --> 00:41:45,000
I want to pick it apart
and pop its perfidious bubble of belief

766
00:41:45,083 --> 00:41:50,292
and get to a truth
I can swallow without choking.

767
00:41:57,708 --> 00:42:02,833
(spluttering) The rafter details
are very fine, though. It's…

768
00:42:04,500 --> 00:42:07,333
Listen… you want to kick me out,
you go right ahead.

769
00:42:07,417 --> 00:42:08,500
No, no.

770
00:42:09,958 --> 00:42:11,625
You're being honest, it's good.

771
00:42:11,708 --> 00:42:15,167
- Telling the truth can be a belly rub.
- (chuckles softly)

772
00:42:15,250 --> 00:42:19,000
Now, I suspect you can't always
be honest with your parishioners.

773
00:42:19,083 --> 00:42:22,250
You can always be honest
by not saying the unhonest part.

774
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:24,875
Yeah. (Laughs)

775
00:42:26,125 --> 00:42:28,125
You're right. It's storytelling.

776
00:42:29,417 --> 00:42:32,958
And this church, it's… it's not medieval.
We're in New York.

777
00:42:33,708 --> 00:42:35,833
It's neo-Gothic 19th century.

778
00:42:35,917 --> 00:42:38,458
It has more in common
with Disneyland than Notre-Dame…

779
00:42:38,542 --> 00:42:40,750
- (chuckles)
- …and the rites and rituals

780
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:43,625
and costumes, all of it.

781
00:42:44,458 --> 00:42:45,875
It's storytelling.

782
00:42:47,333 --> 00:42:48,417
You're right.

783
00:42:51,292 --> 00:42:52,917
I guess the question is

784
00:42:54,625 --> 00:42:56,750
do these stories convince us of a lie?

785
00:42:58,625 --> 00:43:02,708
Or do they resonate with something
deep inside us that's profoundly true?

786
00:43:05,458 --> 00:43:08,125
(clicks tongue) That we can't express
any other way…

787
00:43:10,417 --> 00:43:11,833
Except storytelling.

788
00:43:15,125 --> 00:43:17,333
Touché. Padre.

789
00:43:19,208 --> 00:43:22,833
(laughs, sobs)

790
00:43:25,833 --> 00:43:27,250
- Son.
- I'm sorry.

791
00:43:28,417 --> 00:43:29,625
I just…

792
00:43:30,875 --> 00:43:33,292
I just felt like a priest again,

793
00:43:33,917 --> 00:43:36,042
and now I'm gonna lose that and…

794
00:43:37,000 --> 00:43:39,542
And lose my purpose and I'm frightened, I…

795
00:43:39,625 --> 00:43:42,125
- I don't know how I'll live.
- (door opens)

796
00:43:42,208 --> 00:43:43,292
(Geraldine) Blanc!

797
00:43:44,042 --> 00:43:46,667
Hey! You found him! Is he…

798
00:43:46,750 --> 00:43:49,292
(indistinct police radio chatter)

799
00:43:52,708 --> 00:43:54,458
- (door closes)
- (chuckles)

800
00:43:55,208 --> 00:43:56,208
Who are you?

801
00:43:56,292 --> 00:43:57,917
I probably should have led with this.

802
00:43:58,000 --> 00:44:00,083
My name is Benoit Blanc. I'm a detective.

803
00:44:00,167 --> 00:44:03,583
I've taken an interest
in the murder of Jefferson Wicks.

804
00:44:04,958 --> 00:44:06,792
You're a detective,
so you're with the police?

805
00:44:06,875 --> 00:44:09,083
No, no. I work in a private capacity.

806
00:44:09,167 --> 00:44:12,250
Everyone thinks I did it. I didn't do it.

807
00:44:12,833 --> 00:44:14,875
But in my heart maybe I did,

808
00:44:14,958 --> 00:44:17,458
and the way it happened
was some kind of miracle.

809
00:44:17,542 --> 00:44:18,542
And…

810
00:44:19,042 --> 00:44:21,542
I don't know. I'm lost. I don't know.

811
00:44:22,708 --> 00:44:24,708
Would you allow me to help you?

812
00:44:27,042 --> 00:44:28,125
What?

813
00:44:28,208 --> 00:44:30,958
Your lips are cracked with dehydration.

814
00:44:31,042 --> 00:44:32,458
You haven't slept all night.

815
00:44:33,042 --> 00:44:34,417
You spent it out of doors,

816
00:44:34,500 --> 00:44:37,333
by the state of your pant legs,
on your knees in prayer.

817
00:44:38,083 --> 00:44:41,500
What I see is not a guilty man in torment,

818
00:44:42,250 --> 00:44:45,458
but an innocent man tormented by guilt.

819
00:44:47,083 --> 00:44:48,292
Let me help you.

820
00:44:48,833 --> 00:44:49,708
How?

821
00:44:49,792 --> 00:44:51,708
This was dressed as a miracle.

822
00:44:52,208 --> 00:44:55,625
It's just a murder and I solve murders.

823
00:44:56,125 --> 00:44:57,417
(inhales)

824
00:44:57,500 --> 00:44:59,667
Wait… (hesitates) Were you

825
00:45:00,500 --> 00:45:03,667
the Kentucky Derby thing with the murder
and the guy that they caught

826
00:45:03,750 --> 00:45:05,250
- with the photo-finish camera?
- Yeah.

827
00:45:05,333 --> 00:45:06,417
So you're…

828
00:45:07,250 --> 00:45:08,458
You were on <i>The View.</i>

829
00:45:08,542 --> 00:45:10,167
- Yeah.
- What are you doing here?

830
00:45:10,250 --> 00:45:14,000
I need to come up to speed rapidly
on the events of that Good Friday service

831
00:45:14,083 --> 00:45:17,042
and the goings-on here
at Our Lady of Perpetual Fortitude.

832
00:45:17,708 --> 00:45:20,583
Geraldine has graciously
allowed me access.

833
00:45:20,667 --> 00:45:24,750
If you can spare the day
and accompany me on my investigation,

834
00:45:25,375 --> 00:45:28,542
view the body, trace the murder weapon,
inspect the crime scene,

835
00:45:28,625 --> 00:45:32,125
you are in a unique position to assist me.

836
00:45:33,792 --> 00:45:35,000
The body?

837
00:45:35,583 --> 00:45:37,583
(unsettling string music plays)

838
00:45:38,250 --> 00:45:39,375
(music fades)

839
00:45:40,917 --> 00:45:43,625
Yeah, I've changed my mind.
I don't think I should be here.

840
00:45:44,708 --> 00:45:47,333
Blanc, I'm trusting your process,
but I agree with him.

841
00:45:47,417 --> 00:45:51,500
No, no, no. I want you to get a clear
clinical picture of what happened here.

842
00:45:51,583 --> 00:45:55,667
To see Wicks as he is, a corpse.
Just an empty vessel.

843
00:45:55,750 --> 00:45:59,208
Not the mythologized monster in your mind,
but merely flesh and blood.

844
00:45:59,292 --> 00:46:01,708
Dead from a knife wound we can analyze.

845
00:46:01,792 --> 00:46:04,542
Just… flesh and blood.

846
00:46:05,208 --> 00:46:06,500
Flesh and blood, yeah.

847
00:46:06,583 --> 00:46:09,250
Yes, that's our wiggly wiggly. (Chuckles)

848
00:46:09,833 --> 00:46:12,042
- Yeah. Look at that.
- Please stop doing that.

849
00:46:13,125 --> 00:46:15,792
- Tammy. Would you mind flipping the meat?
- (Tammy) Mm.

850
00:46:15,875 --> 00:46:17,417
- Pancake him. Yeah.
- (Benoit) Mm-hmm.

851
00:46:17,500 --> 00:46:18,542
(Geraldine clears throat)

852
00:46:21,958 --> 00:46:23,583
(Tammy, muffled) One, two, three.

853
00:46:23,667 --> 00:46:24,667
(body thuds)

854
00:46:26,000 --> 00:46:28,458
(panting)

855
00:46:29,500 --> 00:46:32,625
- Hey! Hey.
- No, no, no. I don't belong here.

856
00:46:32,708 --> 00:46:35,083
I can't be in here,
I don't know why you brought me.

857
00:46:35,167 --> 00:46:36,208
- I can't...
- Hey!

858
00:46:37,417 --> 00:46:38,833
If you want absolution,

859
00:46:38,917 --> 00:46:42,375
you want ever to be a priest again,
then you need to go through this with me.

860
00:46:42,458 --> 00:46:46,125
The real killer is out there.
We've got to find him, nail him, I…

861
00:46:46,208 --> 00:46:47,042
I'm sorry.

862
00:46:47,125 --> 00:46:50,042
- I mean, catch him and get your life back.
- (door opens, closes)

863
00:46:50,125 --> 00:46:51,333
Father.

864
00:46:52,083 --> 00:46:54,417
I need to know
you understand the situation.

865
00:46:54,500 --> 00:46:57,833
We are not all buddies
running around trying to solve a case.

866
00:46:57,917 --> 00:46:59,542
You are still a suspect.

867
00:46:59,625 --> 00:47:03,708
The point is, you do not need to be here
without a lawyer, do you understand that?

868
00:47:03,792 --> 00:47:05,500
(intriguing music plays)

869
00:47:05,583 --> 00:47:08,333
(breathing heavily) I didn't do this.

870
00:47:09,583 --> 00:47:11,250
If I can help find out who did,

871
00:47:11,917 --> 00:47:14,458
then I'm in, yeah, let's do it, yeah.

872
00:47:14,542 --> 00:47:15,750
Okay.

873
00:47:16,625 --> 00:47:17,833
The body.

874
00:47:17,917 --> 00:47:19,500
Next the murder weapon.

875
00:47:19,583 --> 00:47:20,667
Then the crime scene.

876
00:47:21,958 --> 00:47:23,375
Stick with me.

877
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:26,167
(intriguing string music plays)

878
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:30,042
(music stops)

879
00:47:30,125 --> 00:47:32,750
I'll tell you something,
I don't even like the devil.

880
00:47:32,833 --> 00:47:36,917
You know, Il Diavolo, it sounds classy,
Italian, that's fine, you know,

881
00:47:37,000 --> 00:47:39,125
then my wife, she buys a devil sign,

882
00:47:39,208 --> 00:47:41,125
then she buys the devil lamps

883
00:47:41,208 --> 00:47:44,792
and then people start, "Oh, hey, get him
a devil thing for the bar, he loves it."

884
00:47:44,875 --> 00:47:48,500
And then, you know, uh,
devil, devil, devil, bang, I don't know.

885
00:47:48,583 --> 00:47:50,958
- (Benoit) But that… But that's it, right?
- (Nikolai) Yeah.

886
00:47:51,042 --> 00:47:52,167
(Benoit) Oh, yeah.

887
00:47:52,250 --> 00:47:55,125
- (rock music plays faintly over speakers)
- (Nikolai) But, uh…

888
00:47:55,208 --> 00:47:58,708
But you know, it wasn't red though,
it's red now, it's paint.

889
00:47:58,792 --> 00:48:01,167
- Yes, yes, freshly painted.
- I hope. Hmm.

890
00:48:01,750 --> 00:48:05,375
They filled it with some kind of plaster
and stuck the blade in that way.

891
00:48:05,458 --> 00:48:07,833
(Jud on recording) <i>I'll do</i>
<i>whatever it takes to save it.</i>

892
00:48:07,917 --> 00:48:11,125
<i>To cut you out like a cancer.</i>

893
00:48:11,208 --> 00:48:14,458
(Nikolai) Hey, hey, hey. Come on, ixnay.
That's not cool. Come on.

894
00:48:14,542 --> 00:48:16,833
The red devil head thing,
that ended up where?

895
00:48:16,917 --> 00:48:21,500
In the church, I threw it at the church
and it broke a window, I don't know why.

896
00:48:21,583 --> 00:48:23,000
- (church bell tolling)
- (gasps)

897
00:48:23,083 --> 00:48:25,292
(Jud) <i>And then</i>
<i>after the Chrism Mass on Monday,</i>

898
00:48:25,375 --> 00:48:27,708
<i>Martha said she found
a small broken window.</i>

899
00:48:27,792 --> 00:48:28,792
Kids.

900
00:48:28,875 --> 00:48:30,292
But nothing else.

901
00:48:30,375 --> 00:48:33,167
(over speakers) <i>♪ I know what's wrong</i>
<i>I don't confuse it ♪</i>

902
00:48:34,542 --> 00:48:36,750
<i>♪ All I'm really trying to say is ♪</i>

903
00:48:36,833 --> 00:48:39,250
<i>♪ Why should the devil have
All the good music? ♪</i>

904
00:48:39,917 --> 00:48:42,333
<i>♪ I feel good every day ♪</i>

905
00:48:44,167 --> 00:48:47,583
<i>♪ Because Jesus is the rock
And he rolled my blues away ♪</i>

906
00:48:48,667 --> 00:48:49,958
(Benoit) Do you see that?

907
00:48:50,042 --> 00:48:52,708
<i>♪ They say to cut my hair
They're driving me insane… ♪</i>

908
00:48:53,583 --> 00:48:54,583
(Benoit) Hmm.

909
00:48:56,083 --> 00:48:57,083
Yeah.

910
00:48:58,625 --> 00:48:59,708
Yeah.

911
00:49:03,375 --> 00:49:05,375
Oh. That is, uh…

912
00:49:07,708 --> 00:49:08,917
Dr. Nat.

913
00:49:10,292 --> 00:49:11,500
Oh, f…

914
00:49:13,792 --> 00:49:15,875
(clears throat) Hey.

915
00:49:17,792 --> 00:49:19,208
Just having some lunch.

916
00:49:20,958 --> 00:49:21,958
(clears throat)

917
00:49:25,750 --> 00:49:29,375
Nat, I… I can come over later
if… if you want to talk.

918
00:49:29,458 --> 00:49:31,583
I don't think… Yeah, I… Yeah.

919
00:49:31,667 --> 00:49:33,583
I don't think I'd prefer that.

920
00:49:33,667 --> 00:49:35,083
I'd prefer not that.

921
00:49:35,833 --> 00:49:39,083
Ah, there it is.
Here's what you did it with. Huh?

922
00:49:39,625 --> 00:49:41,792
- Come on, Nat.
- "Cut him out like a cancer."

923
00:49:41,875 --> 00:49:43,667
You son of a bitch.

924
00:49:43,750 --> 00:49:45,375
- (man) You tell him, Nat.
- Yeah.

925
00:49:46,583 --> 00:49:49,708
- Son of a bitch! Killer priest!
- (door slams)

926
00:49:50,958 --> 00:49:53,583
(intriguing suspenseful music plays)

927
00:50:07,042 --> 00:50:08,458
- (thudding)
- (TV plays indistinctly)

928
00:50:22,167 --> 00:50:23,083
First off,

929
00:50:23,167 --> 00:50:26,125
I owe Detective Elliott a fruit basket
for giving me your number.

930
00:50:26,208 --> 00:50:28,042
I'm just glad to be of service.

931
00:50:28,125 --> 00:50:29,958
Geraldine, you had the foresight

932
00:50:30,042 --> 00:50:32,625
to see that this goes
way beyond normal police work,

933
00:50:32,708 --> 00:50:36,958
this is something
even I have not experienced.

934
00:50:37,042 --> 00:50:41,125
A textbook example
of a perfectly impossible crime.

935
00:50:41,958 --> 00:50:43,583
The stuff of detective fiction.

936
00:50:43,667 --> 00:50:47,000
This should not exist in our real world.

937
00:50:48,042 --> 00:50:49,458
And yet here it is.

938
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:51,417
The holy grail.

939
00:50:52,500 --> 00:50:53,750
(Geraldine) I love the passion.

940
00:50:53,833 --> 00:50:56,333
I just need to feel confident
you know this case is solvable.

941
00:50:56,417 --> 00:50:58,708
(Benoit) I'm incapable
of not solving a crime.

942
00:50:59,458 --> 00:51:01,083
That moment of checkmate

943
00:51:01,958 --> 00:51:05,375
when I take the stage
and unravel my opponent's web…

944
00:51:07,125 --> 00:51:08,417
Oh, you'll see. It's fun!

945
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:11,167
- Great, how do we get there?
- (Benoit) The source,

946
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:14,583
John Dickson Carr's <i>The Hollow Man.</i>

947
00:51:15,375 --> 00:51:17,625
A Golden Age detective novel

948
00:51:17,708 --> 00:51:19,833
and a veritable primer
on the locked-door mystery,

949
00:51:19,917 --> 00:51:21,208
the impossible crime.

950
00:51:22,958 --> 00:51:24,042
Hold up.

951
00:51:24,125 --> 00:51:26,125
(intriguing music plays)

952
00:51:32,375 --> 00:51:35,875
Father Jud, once again,
earning your keep. (Chuckles)

953
00:51:37,542 --> 00:51:40,167
<i>Whose Body?
The Murders in the Rue Morgue.</i>

954
00:51:40,250 --> 00:51:42,667
<i>Roger Ackroyd.
The Murder at the Vicarage.</i>

955
00:51:42,750 --> 00:51:47,958
My God, this is practically
a syllabus for how to commit this crime.

956
00:51:48,458 --> 00:51:50,375
And the whole flock is in this group?

957
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:55,625
Who chose these books?

958
00:51:56,292 --> 00:51:57,292
Oprah.

959
00:51:58,333 --> 00:51:59,333
Oprah.

960
00:51:59,833 --> 00:52:01,917
Martha pulls a theme list
from Oprah's site.

961
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:03,833
Well, this confirms my theory.

962
00:52:04,417 --> 00:52:06,333
The killer certainly imitated

963
00:52:06,417 --> 00:52:09,167
the traditional methods
of a locked-door mystery story.

964
00:52:09,708 --> 00:52:11,917
Which makes things simple.

965
00:52:12,000 --> 00:52:13,208
Book club time.

966
00:52:13,750 --> 00:52:15,167
Come on, kids.

967
00:52:20,667 --> 00:52:21,750
(music fades)

968
00:52:21,833 --> 00:52:24,167
In <i>The Hollow Man,</i>
the detective Gideon Fell

969
00:52:24,250 --> 00:52:29,167
gives a rundown of all possible methods of
a locked-door killing. (Chuckles smugly)

970
00:52:29,250 --> 00:52:31,375
So let's line them up and knock them down.

971
00:52:31,458 --> 00:52:33,583
Possibility number one,

972
00:52:33,667 --> 00:52:37,083
Wicks was stabbed with the knife

973
00:52:37,708 --> 00:52:40,042
before entering the closet.

974
00:52:40,625 --> 00:52:44,042
Father Jud, would you take your place
right where you were?

975
00:52:47,208 --> 00:52:49,333
Wicks completes his sermon.

976
00:52:51,083 --> 00:52:52,750
Any device behind him

977
00:52:52,833 --> 00:52:57,833
capable of propelling
a heavy unbalanced dagger into his back…

978
00:53:00,792 --> 00:53:04,875
Would have been hidden from the camera
and the witnesses in the nave,

979
00:53:05,500 --> 00:53:09,000
but certainly would have been
witnessed by Father Jud.

980
00:53:10,625 --> 00:53:13,667
No, I didn't see
a knife-shooting robot behind him.

981
00:53:13,750 --> 00:53:16,292
(Benoit) No. Possibility one, nixed.

982
00:53:16,375 --> 00:53:18,208
Possibility two…

983
00:53:20,083 --> 00:53:21,500
He was killed

984
00:53:22,000 --> 00:53:23,833
while inside the closet

985
00:53:23,917 --> 00:53:27,542
by someone or something that was

986
00:53:28,292 --> 00:53:30,125
outside the closet.

987
00:53:30,833 --> 00:53:34,417
Like, something shot the knife
into the closet from out here?

988
00:53:34,500 --> 00:53:35,917
No, that's nuts.

989
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:38,000
Nuts and impossible on several fronts.

990
00:53:38,083 --> 00:53:41,083
Possibility two, nixed. Progress!

991
00:53:41,167 --> 00:53:43,250
(sighs) How many more possibilities
are there?

992
00:53:43,333 --> 00:53:45,333
Not many. Three…

993
00:53:46,167 --> 00:53:49,042
He was killed while inside the closet

994
00:53:49,125 --> 00:53:51,750
by a device that was also

995
00:53:53,042 --> 00:53:54,542
inside the closet.

996
00:53:54,625 --> 00:53:56,833
So something was placed beforehand and,

997
00:53:56,917 --> 00:53:58,542
what, triggered with a remote?

998
00:53:58,625 --> 00:54:00,625
Well, these walls are thick,

999
00:54:00,708 --> 00:54:04,125
but a very strong RF signal
could blast through 'em.

1000
00:54:04,875 --> 00:54:07,083
- So where would one hide…
- (lightbulb clinks)

1001
00:54:07,625 --> 00:54:12,042
…a fully functional
remote-controlled knife-propelling device

1002
00:54:12,583 --> 00:54:14,333
in an empty box, mm?

1003
00:54:15,125 --> 00:54:16,875
Wicks fell on his stomach.

1004
00:54:16,958 --> 00:54:19,667
- (intriguing music plays)
- I remember that clangy thud.

1005
00:54:19,750 --> 00:54:20,833
- (thudding)
- (object clangs)

1006
00:54:21,583 --> 00:54:24,083
(Jud) <i>And his face was towards the door,</i>

1007
00:54:24,167 --> 00:54:26,125
so he was standing at the back facing out,

1008
00:54:27,167 --> 00:54:30,583
so the knife must have
somehow come through the back wall.

1009
00:54:31,083 --> 00:54:33,500
Clangy thud. Very good.

1010
00:54:34,042 --> 00:54:35,250
However,

1011
00:54:36,250 --> 00:54:38,125
Rock of Gibraltar.

1012
00:54:38,958 --> 00:54:40,042
What about…

1013
00:54:40,542 --> 00:54:43,125
What about a false wall
that was removed later?

1014
00:54:43,208 --> 00:54:45,167
Go to town, Father Brown.

1015
00:54:45,250 --> 00:54:46,708
(Geraldine) What? No, no.

1016
00:54:46,792 --> 00:54:48,875
My boys would have noticed a fake wall.

1017
00:54:48,958 --> 00:54:51,542
(switch clicking)

1018
00:54:52,750 --> 00:54:56,583
Yeah. And removing it later
would have been no small task.

1019
00:54:58,417 --> 00:55:01,167
Nothing else was found
on the floor of this space, yes?

1020
00:55:01,833 --> 00:55:04,458
Nope. No, just the red thread.

1021
00:55:06,500 --> 00:55:07,917
Wait, what? The…

1022
00:55:08,000 --> 00:55:09,708
The red thread?

1023
00:55:09,792 --> 00:55:11,583
(music rises, fades)

1024
00:55:11,667 --> 00:55:15,292
Two strings of thick red thread
about three inches long, found there

1025
00:55:15,375 --> 00:55:17,375
over by the body next to the hip.

1026
00:55:19,333 --> 00:55:20,917
So, what's possibility four?

1027
00:55:22,375 --> 00:55:25,333
(Martha sobbing)

1028
00:55:26,125 --> 00:55:27,208
Martha?

1029
00:55:28,167 --> 00:55:30,375
- (Martha) Throw them out.
- (Jud) Martha?

1030
00:55:30,458 --> 00:55:31,750
(Martha) Throw them out!

1031
00:55:31,833 --> 00:55:35,083
(sobbing) To walk this holy place

1032
00:55:35,167 --> 00:55:38,500
like some crime scene. Like some

1033
00:55:38,583 --> 00:55:42,208
tawdry police show talking of robots!

1034
00:55:42,792 --> 00:55:46,292
It's not right, Father,
it isn't, it's not right!

1035
00:55:46,375 --> 00:55:47,708
(door opens)

1036
00:55:47,792 --> 00:55:48,833
(door closes)

1037
00:55:48,917 --> 00:55:51,542
Martha, you should go home, get some rest.

1038
00:55:52,083 --> 00:55:53,208
Can I do anything for you?

1039
00:55:54,042 --> 00:55:55,083
Leave.

1040
00:55:55,792 --> 00:55:57,625
No one wants you here anymore.

1041
00:55:58,125 --> 00:56:03,000
You've always hated the Monsignor
and you have nothing but contempt for us.

1042
00:56:03,083 --> 00:56:05,500
- That's not true.
- Murder in your heart…

1043
00:56:05,583 --> 00:56:07,208
- No.
- …blood on your hands

1044
00:56:07,292 --> 00:56:09,292
just like the harlot whore.

1045
00:56:09,375 --> 00:56:12,583
Your original sin has stained this place.

1046
00:56:13,333 --> 00:56:15,083
False priest!

1047
00:56:17,958 --> 00:56:19,542
- Sorry.
- (gasps softly)

1048
00:56:20,375 --> 00:56:22,542
But, yeah,
if finding the facts with that detective

1049
00:56:22,625 --> 00:56:24,792
puts me against you
and this flock then so be it.

1050
00:56:27,583 --> 00:56:28,667
Sorry.

1051
00:56:31,000 --> 00:56:32,958
(music turns melancholic)

1052
00:56:36,542 --> 00:56:38,250
(grunting)

1053
00:56:38,333 --> 00:56:39,458
(music fades)

1054
00:56:39,542 --> 00:56:41,292
Can I use your bag? (Grunting)

1055
00:56:42,792 --> 00:56:44,417
Sure. Go ahead.

1056
00:56:44,500 --> 00:56:47,250
- (continues grunting)
- (TV plays indistinctly)

1057
00:56:47,958 --> 00:56:49,375
Are you okay?

1058
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:53,375
(Jud) <i>A fog was clearing.</i>
<i>This was a puzzle that was solvable.</i>

1059
00:56:53,458 --> 00:56:56,083
<i>The body, the weapon,
the crime scene, robot-knife guns,</i>

1060
00:56:56,167 --> 00:56:59,208
<i>and angles of view
and stone walls and remote controls.</i>

1061
00:56:59,292 --> 00:57:01,583
- (panting)
- (organ music plays on TV)

1062
00:57:01,667 --> 00:57:02,958
<i>And remote controls.</i>

1063
00:57:03,042 --> 00:57:06,500
(commentary on TV) <i>But it's a new day…</i>
<i>The Cubs…</i>

1064
00:57:06,583 --> 00:57:09,333
<i>Who would have been the hero in that game…</i>

1065
00:57:09,417 --> 00:57:11,708
<i>But we saw how that went…</i>

1066
00:57:11,792 --> 00:57:14,208
You didn't listen to the game
during Friday's service.

1067
00:57:14,875 --> 00:57:16,417
On your radio.

1068
00:57:16,500 --> 00:57:20,083
- I would not. Martha doesn't approve.
- (commentary continues)

1069
00:57:21,875 --> 00:57:23,292
So you taped it.

1070
00:57:23,958 --> 00:57:25,042
Yep.

1071
00:57:26,708 --> 00:57:30,792
Okay. I've overlaid a time stamp
taking into account the broadcast delay.

1072
00:57:30,875 --> 00:57:35,125
Dr. Sharp's call to the hospital
happened at 3:47 p.m.

1073
00:57:35,208 --> 00:57:37,625
and 90 seconds before that…

1074
00:57:37,708 --> 00:57:39,000
(keyboard clacking)

1075
00:57:43,625 --> 00:57:46,167
So, several things
could have caused that glitch,

1076
00:57:46,250 --> 00:57:47,625
but to answer your question, yes,

1077
00:57:47,708 --> 00:57:51,208
it is consistent
with a strong burst of RF interference

1078
00:57:51,292 --> 00:57:53,833
that you might get
from a souped-up remote control.

1079
00:57:53,917 --> 00:57:55,500
- (chuckles in relief)
- Damn.

1080
00:57:55,583 --> 00:57:56,500
This is it, right?

1081
00:57:56,583 --> 00:57:58,458
- This triggered the knife robot.
- (sucks teeth)

1082
00:57:58,542 --> 00:58:01,333
This has got to be it.
You can… You can solve it now, right?

1083
00:58:01,417 --> 00:58:03,833
Did you, um…
Did you sync this up with Cy's footage?

1084
00:58:03,917 --> 00:58:07,667
I did, yeah. The iPhone video
has a time stamp. It's very precise.

1085
00:58:07,750 --> 00:58:09,750
- Okay. Can you show us please?
- Yeah.

1086
00:58:09,833 --> 00:58:10,875
Show us what?

1087
00:58:10,958 --> 00:58:13,833
(Jud) <i>♪ On which hung</i>
<i>The salvation of the world ♪</i>

1088
00:58:13,917 --> 00:58:15,792
- (thudding)
- (object clangs)

1089
00:58:16,875 --> 00:58:17,958
(Jud) <i>Monsignor?</i>

1090
00:58:23,917 --> 00:58:24,917
(keyboard clacks)

1091
00:58:29,333 --> 00:58:30,792
- Oh.
- (Benoit groans)

1092
00:58:30,875 --> 00:58:33,000
So when the RF burst happened,

1093
00:58:33,083 --> 00:58:35,917
he was already on the ground
with a knife in his back

1094
00:58:36,542 --> 00:58:38,875
and Father Jud was staring right at him.

1095
00:58:39,375 --> 00:58:41,167
How does that work?

1096
00:58:41,250 --> 00:58:43,958
Well, for the knife robot, it doesn't.

1097
00:58:44,042 --> 00:58:46,625
So… this was nothing?

1098
00:58:46,708 --> 00:58:47,625
(Benoit) No, no.

1099
00:58:47,708 --> 00:58:51,042
This was very much something.
(inhales deeply)

1100
00:58:51,125 --> 00:58:53,958
We have all the pieces
laid out before us now.

1101
00:58:54,042 --> 00:58:55,167
- We do?
- Yeah.

1102
00:58:55,250 --> 00:58:58,458
Consider the origin
of the devil's head, the red thread,

1103
00:58:58,542 --> 00:59:01,958
the clangy clunk,
the timing of the RF remote.

1104
00:59:02,958 --> 00:59:04,375
It all lines up.

1105
00:59:05,500 --> 00:59:07,708
(chuckles uncertainly)
So give us the answer.

1106
00:59:08,917 --> 00:59:10,208
I can't.

1107
00:59:11,667 --> 00:59:15,208
You said if you had all the pieces,
you would have an answer.

1108
00:59:15,292 --> 00:59:16,542
(Benoit) I know. And yet,

1109
00:59:16,625 --> 00:59:18,708
with all the pieces on the table,

1110
00:59:18,792 --> 00:59:21,958
this crime still truly appears

1111
00:59:22,750 --> 00:59:23,958
impossible.

1112
00:59:24,792 --> 00:59:26,500
You told me you could solve this.

1113
00:59:26,583 --> 00:59:28,458
(solemn music plays)

1114
00:59:28,542 --> 00:59:30,042
That's what you do.

1115
00:59:31,083 --> 00:59:33,667
(sighs) I put my faith in you.

1116
00:59:33,750 --> 00:59:37,250
Oh, God. Oh, God. (Sighs)

1117
00:59:37,333 --> 00:59:41,375
(Benoit) You haven't slept in 36 hours.
I think it's bedtime. (Clears throat)

1118
00:59:41,458 --> 00:59:42,542
Yeah.

1119
00:59:43,167 --> 00:59:45,875
It's been a hell of a day.
Good night, Father Jud.

1120
00:59:45,958 --> 00:59:47,500
Come on. I'll drive you back.

1121
00:59:48,375 --> 00:59:50,375
(intriguing music plays)

1122
00:59:56,833 --> 00:59:59,375
- (music fades)
- You know, you're right.

1123
00:59:59,458 --> 01:00:02,083
This can't be impossible.

1124
01:00:03,333 --> 01:00:05,167
There must be a piece missing.

1125
01:00:06,458 --> 01:00:08,458
And I think I know where to find it.

1126
01:00:09,042 --> 01:00:11,792
See, I think there's something in there,
in your head,

1127
01:00:11,875 --> 01:00:14,292
that I need to solve this case.

1128
01:00:14,958 --> 01:00:17,792
And if I can't shake it loose, I'm sorry,

1129
01:00:18,333 --> 01:00:20,750
but I'm gonna have to
go in there and get it.

1130
01:00:23,250 --> 01:00:25,167
Okay, you're freaking me out now.

1131
01:00:27,250 --> 01:00:29,500
- I don't… I don't understand.
- (door closes)

1132
01:00:29,583 --> 01:00:32,542
I don't get how this will help.
You want me to write the story?

1133
01:00:32,625 --> 01:00:34,833
Yes, the story.

1134
01:00:34,917 --> 01:00:37,625
The story of the Reverend Wicks' murder.

1135
01:00:37,708 --> 01:00:38,792
Monsignor Wicks.

1136
01:00:38,875 --> 01:00:40,708
Monsignor Jalapeño. I don't care.

1137
01:00:40,792 --> 01:00:44,208
I need to see his murder and the events
leading up to it through your eyes.

1138
01:00:44,292 --> 01:00:45,167
Starting where?

1139
01:00:45,250 --> 01:00:46,333
Oh, wherever you like.

1140
01:00:46,417 --> 01:00:49,625
Just keep it interesting,
keep it moving and spare no detail.

1141
01:00:49,708 --> 01:00:51,000
Blanc, I'm not a writer.

1142
01:00:51,083 --> 01:00:53,083
Take your time. Oh.

1143
01:00:53,625 --> 01:00:56,375
- (chuckles) I'll be quite comfortable.
- (cork squeaks)

1144
01:00:56,458 --> 01:00:57,667
- (glass clinks)
- (liquid pours)

1145
01:00:57,750 --> 01:00:59,250
(Benoit humming)

1146
01:00:59,333 --> 01:01:01,333
(intriguing suspenseful music plays)

1147
01:01:04,042 --> 01:01:07,042
(Jud) <i>So I've spent the past hour</i>
<i>doing exactly that</i>

1148
01:01:07,542 --> 01:01:10,750
<i>and now I'll put down this pen
and hand it to you</i>

1149
01:01:11,250 --> 01:01:12,458
<i>and I guess, wait</i>

1150
01:01:12,542 --> 01:01:14,458
<i>while you read my story</i>

1151
01:01:14,542 --> 01:01:17,500
<i>of the murder
of Monsignor Jefferson Wicks.</i>

1152
01:01:18,000 --> 01:01:20,000
(intriguing suspenseful music continues)

1153
01:01:25,208 --> 01:01:26,250
(sighs softly)

1154
01:01:31,792 --> 01:01:32,792
(sighs)

1155
01:01:34,292 --> 01:01:35,292
(music fades)

1156
01:01:44,083 --> 01:01:45,292
Why'd you do it?

1157
01:01:48,625 --> 01:01:50,125
Better question is,

1158
01:01:50,708 --> 01:01:53,417
why'd I think I could lie to you
and get away with it?

1159
01:01:53,500 --> 01:01:56,125
Oh, no, you didn't lie.
I knew you wouldn't.

1160
01:01:57,000 --> 01:01:59,375
You just didn't say
the dishonest part out loud.

1161
01:01:59,458 --> 01:02:01,167
(suspenseful music plays)

1162
01:02:03,125 --> 01:02:07,625
"When I joined the others outside,
the police were just arriving."

1163
01:02:07,708 --> 01:02:09,917
- (distant sirens wailing)
- (suspenseful music continues)

1164
01:02:10,000 --> 01:02:11,625
<i>"Joined the others outside."</i>

1165
01:02:12,125 --> 01:02:14,125
<i>So you stayed inside.</i>

1166
01:02:14,208 --> 01:02:17,042
<i>So you were the only person
with unobserved access</i>

1167
01:02:17,125 --> 01:02:21,500
<i>to the utility closet after the murder,
but before the police searched it.</i>

1168
01:02:23,542 --> 01:02:25,042
Why? I mean… (scoffs)

1169
01:02:28,000 --> 01:02:29,292
Why protect him?

1170
01:02:29,375 --> 01:02:31,375
I didn't do it to protect Wicks.

1171
01:02:33,042 --> 01:02:35,375
I did it to save the people
who believe in him

1172
01:02:35,458 --> 01:02:36,875
just a little disillusionment.

1173
01:02:36,958 --> 01:02:39,792
Well, surely everyone knew.

1174
01:02:39,875 --> 01:02:43,375
<i>I mean, it would have been
on his breath after every Mass.</i>

1175
01:02:43,458 --> 01:02:46,417
<i>"Fortified himself."
Oh, clever wording there.</i>

1176
01:02:46,500 --> 01:02:48,667
<i>But everyone must have known.</i>

1177
01:02:48,750 --> 01:02:50,875
The spirit really moved him today, huh?

1178
01:02:51,542 --> 01:02:52,958
No, not everyone.

1179
01:02:53,458 --> 01:02:54,542
Sam.

1180
01:02:55,333 --> 01:02:57,542
The one good person in this whole place.

1181
01:02:57,625 --> 01:02:59,625
Getting sober saved his life.

1182
01:03:01,042 --> 01:03:04,875
<i>I knew the press and police were there.
Why let that be part of the story?</i>

1183
01:03:06,458 --> 01:03:07,292
(clangs)

1184
01:03:07,375 --> 01:03:09,792
(Jud) <i>Wicks had been stabbed.</i>

1185
01:03:09,875 --> 01:03:13,625
<i>I don't know how or by who,
but I… but I knew he'd been stabbed.</i>

1186
01:03:13,708 --> 01:03:15,708
<i>So this had nothing to do with it.</i>

1187
01:03:15,792 --> 01:03:17,917
<i>It was an impulse.</i>

1188
01:03:18,000 --> 01:03:20,542
A little storytelling to protect my flock.

1189
01:03:20,625 --> 01:03:22,250
Oh, bullshit!

1190
01:03:22,333 --> 01:03:26,417
And in protecting their bubble of belief,
you have shielded a killer!

1191
01:03:29,000 --> 01:03:30,625
Where is that flask?

1192
01:03:31,917 --> 01:03:34,833
- (objects clattering)
- (Jud) Shit!

1193
01:03:37,458 --> 01:03:39,667
Shit. It's not here. (Panting)

1194
01:03:39,750 --> 01:03:40,750
(music fades)

1195
01:03:40,833 --> 01:03:41,917
Blanc.

1196
01:03:42,875 --> 01:03:44,083
I'm sorry.

1197
01:03:44,708 --> 01:03:46,708
He was stabbed, so I didn't think...

1198
01:03:46,792 --> 01:03:48,917
No. You did not think.

1199
01:03:49,000 --> 01:03:51,292
But we're into the woods now,
so you better start.

1200
01:03:51,375 --> 01:03:52,625
(panting)

1201
01:03:53,292 --> 01:03:55,125
Someone broke into my room.

1202
01:03:56,125 --> 01:03:57,417
It's just hitting me.

1203
01:03:57,958 --> 01:04:00,792
This is devious,
it's calculated against me.

1204
01:04:00,875 --> 01:04:03,208
And now you see
the enemy we're up against.

1205
01:04:05,125 --> 01:04:08,000
You have listened to this flock's stories
with empathy and grace,

1206
01:04:08,083 --> 01:04:09,833
but we're done with that now.

1207
01:04:09,917 --> 01:04:11,542
We've wasted enough time.

1208
01:04:11,625 --> 01:04:14,042
Tomorrow we use
the gathering at the burial

1209
01:04:14,125 --> 01:04:15,750
to question them all together.

1210
01:04:15,833 --> 01:04:18,667
We must discover what happened that night.

1211
01:04:19,750 --> 01:04:23,833
And what this flock
of wicked wolves is hiding.

1212
01:04:24,875 --> 01:04:28,958
(snoring)

1213
01:04:29,042 --> 01:04:30,042
(Benoit) Mm.

1214
01:04:31,292 --> 01:04:33,042
(sighs)

1215
01:04:40,917 --> 01:04:42,333
Betrayed.

1216
01:04:43,333 --> 01:04:44,542
Beaten.

1217
01:04:46,667 --> 01:04:47,875
Mocked.

1218
01:04:48,917 --> 01:04:50,125
Pierced.

1219
01:04:50,208 --> 01:04:52,208
(tense music plays)

1220
01:04:52,292 --> 01:04:53,375
Murdered.

1221
01:04:53,458 --> 01:04:54,583
(thudding)

1222
01:04:55,375 --> 01:04:56,583
(machine whirs)

1223
01:04:57,375 --> 01:05:00,000
<i>And left in a hole to rot.</i>

1224
01:05:00,917 --> 01:05:02,000
<i>To be forgotten.</i>

1225
01:05:03,208 --> 01:05:06,042
<i>As with our Savior, so with the church.</i>

1226
01:05:06,708 --> 01:05:10,208
<i>Our church is assailed
by wicked modernity,</i>

1227
01:05:10,292 --> 01:05:12,500
<i>by the enemies of God.</i>

1228
01:05:12,583 --> 01:05:14,583
<i>The harlot whores,</i>

1229
01:05:14,667 --> 01:05:18,000
<i>the vermin who would oppress and silence</i>

1230
01:05:18,083 --> 01:05:20,500
<i>and bar us from our rightful place</i>

1231
01:05:20,583 --> 01:05:23,208
<i>as the rulers
of a Christian nation of faith.</i>

1232
01:05:23,708 --> 01:05:26,792
And even as I stand before you
as a warrior of Christ,

1233
01:05:26,875 --> 01:05:30,708
